Someone wrote:

>> But there aren't any cylinders, heads, or sectors as per typical
>> hard drives.

Then someone else wrote:

>I already know about that.  That is physical one track on CD.  
>However, I mean that logical sectors, cylinders, etc. that one spiral 
>track can be broken down into.  On my OpenVMS installation CD, how I can 
>find number of LBNs when "online" and "get unit status" command are
>issued on my RQDX3 emulator?

I thought you knew about that, but you did ask.
Tracks and cylinders are never visible to the host OS on SCSI
disks.  The only thing is the total number of blocks.  Some
(many, all, most?) magnetic SCSI disks allow the blocksize to be
specified and the disk reformatted, but most now use 512 bytes.

CDROM's use 2048 byte blocks, but as someone said in this 
thread, some drives can return them as 512 byte blocks.

I don't have the SCSI CCS list here, so I won't say more about it.

It might be that you have to get the number of blocks from the
file system itself.  

-- glen


