Hi Tim,

sword7@speakeasy.org wrote:
> 
> In alt.sys.pdp11 Eric Smith <eric-no-spam-for-me@brouhaha.com> wrote:
> > The RRD40, and CD-ROMs in general, don't HAVE a geometry in the sense
> > that disk drives are said to.  In mode 1 (the normal mode), they are
> > a linear array of 2048-byte blocks numbered from 0 to n-1.  The original
> > CD-ROM spec only allowed for 74 minute discs.  Each second translates to
> > 75 blocks, so the maximum blocks per disc was 74*60*75 = 333,000.
> 
> > In practice, most 74-minute CD-R blanks had somewhat more than 74 minutes,
> > and of course now many blanks are actually specified as 80 minutes.
> > 80 minutes is 80*60*75 blocks = 360,000.
> 
> > Some CD-ROM drives can be placed in a 512-byte sector mode for use with
> > older Unix systems.  Instead of 0..n-1 2K blocks, they have 0..4n-1
> > 512 byte blocks.
> 
> > But there aren't any cylinders, heads, or sectors as per typical
> > hard drives.
> 
> I already know about that.  That is physical one track on CD.
> However, I mean that logical sectors, cylinders, etc. that one spiral
> track can be broken down into.  On my OpenVMS installation CD, how I can
> find number of LBNs when "online" and "get unit status" command are
> issued on my RQDX3 emulator?

The layout of the end message packets for these are:

ONLINE response:

  31          16       8          0
  +-------------------------------+
  |  command reference number     |
  +-------------------------------+
  |  reserved  |  unit number     |
  +-------------------------------+
  |  status    | flags | end code |
  +-------------------------------+
  | unit flags | multi-unit code  +
  +-------------------------------+
  |            reserved           |
  +-------------------------------+
  |            unit               |
  +-----                      ----+
  |          identifier           |
  +-------------------------------+
  |     media type identifier     |
  +-------------------------------+
  |  reserved  |    reserved      |
  +-------------------------------+
  |         unit size             |  <- size in LBN
  +-------------------------------+
  |     volume serial number      |
  +-------------------------------+


GET UNIT STATUS:

  31      24     16      8          0
  +---------------------------------+
  |    command reference number     |
  +---------------------------------+
  |  reserved    | unit number      |
  +---------------------------------+
  |  status      | flags | end code |
  +---------------------------------+
  | unit flags   | multi-unit code  |
  +---------------------------------+
  |            reserved             |
  +---------------------------------+
  |            unit                 |
  +-----                      ------+
  |          identifier             |
  +---------------------------------+
  |     media type identifier       |
  +---------------------------------+
  |  reserved    |  shadow unit     |
  +---------------------------------+
  | group size   |  track size      |
  +---------------------------------+
  |  reserved    |  cylinder size   |
  +---------------------------------+
  |copies | RBNs |  RCT size        |
  +---------------------------------+

Note that the GET UNIT STATUS response does NOT return
the drive's size in LBNs. As I indicated before the 
end message does not have to contain values for the
drive size fields.

