> Alan Greig wrote:
> > Nothing in Doug Williams presentation on the Alpha-IPF switch
> > suggested that the Alpha could not continue to outpace the IA64 for at
> > least the next three years. In fact, when pressed, Doug admitted that
> > they could probably keep Alpha ahead of the game (but not by much he
> > said) for between five and ten years had they executed the EV8 plan.

"Nic Clews" wrote
> Alan,
>
> Do you recall where Doug talked (briefly) about patent infringement? He
> spoke about the original Digital-Intel fiasco, and did say two of the
> patents were his, but he also, sort-of under his breath, mumbled
> something around EV8.
>
> It was also when he was mentioning Alpha engineers moving [of their own
> accord] to AMD and Intel, among other chip manufacturers. Personally, I
> guess that is another factor, with the chip business being so
> competitive. I doubt Alpha engineers wouldn't be so mercenary as not to
> turn down a nice paycheque, and didn't Doug indicate / intimate this had
> been happening? ...

The patent settlement between Digital and Intel, in which Intel
purchased the StrongArm product & team and Alpha foundry, and
committed to fab Alpha chips for awhile, included a clause where
Intel committed to also give jobs to Digital's Alpha chip design
team in the future, if and when Digital decided to drop that effort.
Compaq inherited those off-loading rights.  That clause is still
in effect this year, but expires in 2002 or 2003.

I imagine this colored Compaq's decisions about how and when to
scale back on Alpha chip design efforts, and maybe on how to
partner with other companies.  It allowed most folks to feel
somewhat better about firing so many good people.
But it likely wasn't a dominant factor; I can't imagine Capellas
letting personal welfare of one team affect the business plan.

It's also interesting that HP announced a similar offloading of
their PA-RISC and IPF chip design teams to Intel, sometime
between Compaq's Alphacide announcement and the joint
merger announcement.

For HP to unconditionally burn their PA-RISC bridges now
says something about their expectation about future IPF
performance & schedules, based on far better insider
information than the public has.  They've been burnt by
Intel many times in the Merced effort, but are being confident
about future parts anyhow.  Are HP's system designers
continuing to be gullible about Intel roadmaps, or do
they know things are turning out far better than the
pessimists say?   Compaq has made identical decisions
(and began those before any merger flirting).  Either
both HP and Compaq are gullible about Intel's roadmap
claims, or neither is.










