Tracy,

Several things about Compaq:

1. Like nearly all name-brand manufacturers, Compaq tends to bury information
about older computers.  With acquired computers like the DEC ones, I think
they've tried extra hard.

2. If Compaq does not have a corporate policy to plead complete ignorance about
the DEC gear, their policy of not retaining many of the people knowledgeable in
DEC products made them almost completely ignorant.

3. Complete ignorance of DEC products came to Compaq when it sold off all the
ex-DEC hardware inventory, new and used.  Now and again, you'll find an ex-DEC
engineer in Compaq who has maintained his/her personal treasure of DEC info.  A
lot of these folks read this newsgroup and willingly share their DEC info, in
defiance of the official Compaq policy of ignorance of DEC gear. 

4. AFAIK, there is no industry-wide utility software to scan thru an EISA bus
computer and, at minimum, display the EISA IDs of all the EISA boards.  There
are several very useful ones for PCI cards, but EISA never achieved any great
popularity beyond the server realm, because EISA was such a pain in the ass to
use.   As you've found out.  The EISA IDs are/were pre-assigned by MCS with a
manufacturer prefix (CPQ) and a four digit number.  MCS has abandoned the EISA
business, and their website is no help any more.  Their FTP site has some EISA
remnants, but nothing useful for you.

Having said all that, I think there are two possible courses of action:

1. Better identify the board you now have, and dive back into the Compaq web
site, or possibly do a Google search.  Relevant bits of information are the info
displayed by the board's BIOS when the system powers up, the Compaq part number
stickered on the board, and any manufacturing dates you can decipher.  It's
possible one of the zillion Compaq "Service Paqs" has an EISA config utility
program plus the config files you need.  Surely, Compaq used its own board in
its own computers, too.  The board was designed back when Compaq thought they
were in the hardware board design and manufacturing business, instead of relying
more upon commonly available cards such as Adaptec SCSIs, for example.  Compaq
designed even stranger pieces of hardware, too.  An example is the combo
VGA-SCSI card, EISA, as I recall.  Also an EISA Token Ring or Ethernet card,
with a little plug-in module for one or the other.  And their early server
motherboards and power supplies.  Wow!

2. Dump the card in favor of a comparable PCI card or maybe a comparable EISA
card for which you can obtain an EISA CFG file.

That's about all I can suggest... Ben Myers

