has
> happened.

You missed the distinction I was making between Alpha's *credibility* (the
feeling that current Alphas, and new Alphas when they appear, will be worthy
platforms to run on) and its *momentum* (which wasn't that great to start
with and took a fatal hit five months ago - but could pick up again if a new
corporate shoulder were solidly placed behind it).  If people had lost faith
in Alpha's *quality*, that might well truly be irrecoverable.

But let's assume you meant what I'm calling 'momentum'.  You should note
that your comments apply at least as much to VMS as they do to Alpha and
Tru64.  Unlike Alpha and Tru64, the common perception is that VMS died years
ago, which is why few ISVs will bother porting their VMS applications to
Itanic (since there's no reason to believe that its customer base will even
match the current one:  Compaq's corporate shoulder has never been behind
VMS, and this shows absolutely no sign of changing).  Even before June 25th
the VMS ISV base was dwindling, and the external perception of VMS's
viability (among those few who still had any such perception at all) has
gone down-hill dramatically since then.

That's why nothing short of a corporate brain-transplant has any hope of
even restoring VMS's perceived viability to its pre-June-25th levels:
current management has a far too consistent track record in its handling of
VMS for any change by them to be considered at all worthy of trust (which,
for that matter, applies to just about any other stance they might take).

...

> > I'll spell out the current problem once more, because you still seem to
