>
> "A" above is flat out not true.  I have seen the internal numbers and what
> the Alpha servers have been contributing in profits over the last several
> years is significantly below two other groups.  Sorry but the information
I
> saw I can't release.

You don't have to, because it's pretty obvious:  service and storage, when
viewed in the right light (i.e., as business units that don't owe a large
portion of their revenue to Alpha's existence), can appear that way.  Or if
you want to talk margins the Tandem division may qualify (though since they
were indicated as bringing in less than 1/3 the total revenue that Alpha
systems did as of a Compaq presentation last March they clearly don't match
Alpha's total profits).

The problem with treating service and storage as separate from Alpha is that
without Alpha they'd be far smaller than they are.  It is, however, possible
to compare Alpha-system-related profit to Tandem-system-related profit and
industry-standard-server-related profit (and we won't even bother with
PC-related profit, since it's effectively non-existent).  And in that
apples-to-apples comparison, Alpha stands far above the others (for the last
several years, which was *your* choice of time-scale; the only time ISS
profits have threatened it was during the dot-com boom of Y2K).

- bill





