sword7@speakeasy.org writes:
> I am looking for complete disk geometries information about RRD40 CD-ROM
> drive.  Does anyone have info or specs about RRD40 CD drive?

I seem to recall that you've asked this question before, but here we
go again...

The RRD40, and CD-ROMs in general, don't HAVE a geometry in the sense
that disk drives are said to.  In mode 1 (the normal mode), they are
a linear array of 2048-byte blocks numbered from 0 to n-1.  The original
CD-ROM spec only allowed for 74 minute discs.  Each second translates to
75 blocks, so the maximum blocks per disc was 74*60*75 = 333,000.

In practice, most 74-minute CD-R blanks had somewhat more than 74 minutes,
and of course now many blanks are actually specified as 80 minutes.
80 minutes is 80*60*75 blocks = 360,000.

Some CD-ROM drives can be placed in a 512-byte sector mode for use with
older Unix systems.  Instead of 0..n-1 2K blocks, they have 0..4n-1
512 byte blocks.

But there aren't any cylinders, heads, or sectors as per typical
hard drives.


