In article <qhadx5krfw.fsf@ruckus.brouhaha.com>, Eric Smith <eric-no-spam-for-me@brouhaha.com> writes:

:The RRD40, and CD-ROMs in general, don't HAVE a geometry in the sense
:that disk drives are said to.  

  To be pedantic, disk drives also no longer have a traditional disk
  geometry. :-)

:But there aren't any cylinders, heads, or sectors as per typical
:hard drives.

  The cylinders/tracks/sectors geometry of disk drives is now typically
  synthesized, with more sectors existing per track as the track length 
  increases toward the outside of the platter surface -- this makes better 
  use of the platter surface.  Without this technique, disk capacity was 
  limited to the maximum density of the innermost track.

  Most applications only care about referencing the relative block number, 
  and the disk drives provide this quite nicely -- most application code 
  does not care about the vagaries of disk geometry.

  I would not be surprised to find a disk using one really long spiral 
  track.  Oh, wait, that's a CD. :-)

