I spoke to Steve Shirron, who authored the RQDX3 firmware.  There
never was a CD on the RQDX3; CD's were always on SCSI controllers.
VMS probably won't barf at at RRD40 on an RQDX3.  You can set the
geometry arbitrarily, as long as the LBN's come out right.

A UNIX dd rip of the VMS hobbyist CD is 681,578,496 bytes long, or
1331208 512-byte blocks.  You can round up a bit to fit the previous
calculation of 333,000 2048-byte = 1332000 512-byte sectors.  You can
make up cylinders, tracks, groups, etc to your liking, as long as all
the fields are 16b or less.  I'm using 128 sectors per track, 1
surface, and 10400 cylinders.

/Bob

