1.  They will care because *verifying* that the recompile of their
applications (and any recompiled components they obtained from others) was
all it took takes non-negligible effort.

2.  They will care because Compaq will have spent 3 years preoccupied with
the porting effort rather than adding *real* value to VMS (and ISVs will
have been diverted by such activity as well).

3.  They will care because the risk that the transition will *not* be as
smooth as you posit and/or that Itanic will continue to suck so badly that
cost-effective platforms simply cannot be based on it will have
significantly reduced VMS's market (and ISV base) over the 3 years before
such risks have been proved not to exist (*if* we accept your assumptions to
this effect) and therefore VMS will have a significantly smaller customer
base to fund its future development (if there is such) or even existence.

And, of course, for some non-zero number of installations, recompiling is
not an option in any event - and even if running via a binary translator is
effective that too will require verification effort.  And some other
non-zero number of installations will have to choose between running
unsupported cluster mixes that include VAXen and reconfiguring to avoid
them.  And I'm sure there are some other minorities that will be affected in
other ways that don't pop into my mind (but likely can be filled in by
others here):  for a system with as small a user population as VMS already
has, it doesn't take too many such minorities to add up to something
significant.

Those are the down-sides when we *grant* your assumptions.  The actual
potential down-sides are far worse.  And there's *no* real up-side:  at best
(and again granting your assumptions rather than using a less biased crystal
ball), by 2005 - 6 people will be able to buy hardware to run VMS that's
somewhat less expensive for the same performance than would otherwise have
been available, but hardware cost is not (at least according to several
surveys that used to be on the Compaq Web site) a dominant concern for VMS
customers anyway (very-low-end applications possibly excepted, but *that's*
a whole 'nother subject).

