> performance and scaling.

*If* they're willing to run on a dead-end architecture that won't continue
to ride the industry performance curve.  Some existing Alpha users may well
do that and plan to jump to POWER4 when EV7x runs out of steam.  New users,
however, will opt for POWER4 up front to avoid the need to migrate there
later.

>
> But a great deal of people will be very happy with IA64 performance.  It
> will get significantly better over time.

If it didn't, the product would vanish without a trace.  If it does,
performance might actually rise to the level of mediocrity (as long as you
don't mind the power dissipation) - and indeed some people will probably be
'very happy' with that (just as they are with Windows, not knowing anything
better).

  And yes, throwing additional
> IA64's at the problem will equal the performance they "might" have gotten
on
> Alpha.

As long as they're not interested in any of the activities you noted above.

Even given that, there's still the question of whether you can sell a
2N-processor Itanic system for noticeably less than an N-processor Alpha
system.  The answer for N = 1 is likely 'yes'.  For N = 2 I'd be more
inclined to say 'maybe', and the more N rises the more skeptical I become.

And that's just the *hardware* pricing.  Unless Compaq starts giving away
large-N VMS licenses, when you add that to the hardware price whatever
difference existed just became considerably less significant.

