> either it has to work, or the clustering software needs to behave
> differently depending on what other nodes are in the cluster.  To
> make the clustering software sensitive to the other nodes would be
> a fair amount of work for no conceivable benefit.
>
> Imagine this situation:  1) Boot up a cluster consisting of a bunch
> of Alpha nodes.  2) Boot a VAX into the cluster.  Works fine.  Now
> imagine a second situation:  1) Boot up a cluster consisting of a
> bunch of Alpha nodes.  2) Boot an IPF into the cluster.  Should also
> work fine.  So how do all the Alphas at initial boot time know
> which situation will prevail, and start doing all the stuff that
> won't work with VAXes?

Not that I have the slightest clue in this area, but now that you've posed
the question the following kludge just popped into my head (though far be it
from me to endorse it...):

People a while ago mentioned a likely problem with existing DCL usage that
might assume that if something was not an Alpha then it must be a VAX
(IIRC).  If one prohibited fully-integrated clusters than such usage could
be made to work transparently in either an Alpha-Itanic or an Alpha-VAX
cluster.  Of course, a different (and I'd say cleaner) way would be to
define an Itanic as an Alpha for that purpose and establish a new way to
differentiate between them.

That kind of thing *could* possibly motivate the non-support of
fully-integrated clusters.  But it's hard to imagine that there wouldn't be
cleaner ways around it, and as I said initially this is speculation of the
most ignorant kind (to a degree which would be deplorable if I hadn't
already apologized twice for it - but it's pleasant to do something other
than argue at least once in a while).

- bill





