hopefully there is something there:

One part I do remember was fiddling with the 'qsbypass.scr' script.
Changing all instances of 'fat' to
[mop|tftp|ewa?|something-else-which-I-cannot-rememeber]. 

Hmmmmmmm.... I think it went something like this. You'll need to switch
the machine to SRM mode if not already:

0. Check the current firmware. 3.8 is the latest (last) version. If
you're already there, then this is pointless.

1. Get the Multia firmware update and create the floppies 'normally'
(ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/Alpha/firmware/archive/multia/)

2. Copy the files from the udp-created floppy to the intended MOP/TFTP
boot directory on your boot host. I think the fsl (FailSafe Loader)
image is corrupted.

3. Setup MOP/TFTP to use fwupdate.exe as the boot file.

4. Modify the 'qsbypass.scr' file as noted... This may take some
hacking.

5. boot ewa? (whatever the ethernet device is)

6. Pray...

Or did I give up on that and hack this bit??

>>> update -path fat:srm.exe/dva0 -target srmflash
-----------------^^^---------^^^^

Perhaps some former DEC Multia engineer can help by cleaning this up a
bit? :)

