lines Compaq has into yet another struggling one,  b) in the process likely
cost more money than eliminating development will save over the same period
(let alone the cost in future sales),   c) cut VMS's its nascent sales
up-turn off at the knees (thus putting yet another stake into the heart of
its already-fragile long-term viability)  d) did similar damage to Tru64
(though the merger now shows signs of turning that lingering illness into a
more precipitous demise), and  e) highlighted the fact that Compaq
management are incompetent weasels with whom at least many people would not
choose to do business given any reasonable alternative.

The future 'blade' architecture you've described does not change any of the
above in the slightest - in fact, it supports it.  Because Compaq *has*
elected to make the blades support both Alpha and Itanic engines, they will
indeed benefit from any volume market that Itanic might provide, and thus
the only difference in cost between an Alpha-based blade server and an
Itanic-based blade server will be the per-processor cost, which we
apparently both agree is not a major contributor to overall server price.

(I said 5 months ago when this brouhaha began that there was no reason that
Alpha-based platforms couldn't take advantage of all the 'industry-standard'
components that comparable Itanic platforms used, and this bi-sexual 'blade'
design just takes that to its logical extreme.  As long as the design
doesn't cripple Alpha's strengths, it's win-win for both, and the only
reason I suggested earlier that it *would* cripple Alpha was because that
was the only explanation for your assertion that somehow for a given level
of performance the Itanic version would be less expensive than the Alpha
version.)

Let's go over that once again, so that there's at least a chance you will
understand it:  Compaq has ensured that the only difference in cost between
an Alpha blade system and an Itanic blade system will be the cost of the
processors (or even less, if the Itanic system requires extra glue elements
that the Alpha system by virtue of its on-chip features does not).  That
difference will be only a small percentage of overall server cost - assuming
that both systems have the same number of processors.  But in that case the
Alpha-based system will have about twice the performance of the Itanic-based
system - or, if you prefer, will require only about half the blades of the
Itanic system and therefore cost considerably *less* than the Itanic-based
system of comparable performance.

I have no problem if Compaq can sell zillions of Itanic systems of that type
(though strongly doubt that it will, because I doubt that they will turn out
to be competitive with the other options that will be out there, principally
POWER4 and Hammer).  What I have a problem with is not maintaining the Alpha
development that would have allowed them to offer right alongside those
Itanic systems the option of buying more cost-effective (though less
'industry-standard') Alpha systems - *even though each Alpha processor in
those systems carried the full burden of its development cost and thus
funded it fully*.  *That's* what's so stupid - not only because it's lost
opportunity for Compaq, but because the other effects of killing Alpha
*will* be felt by customers (as described previously) and the incredibly
sleazy and perfidious manner in which the murder was handled *will* have
consequences on Compaq's future in general.

None of those are marginal calls.  Alpha's per-processor performance is
superior and would have only become more so over the time period for which
any real information is available.  Significant percentages of customers are
already significantly upset at  a) the disruption they foresee in their
operations and  b) the way Compaq went about this.  And Alpha's
profitability was well-established and relatively constant, despite the
neglect of its owner (so this argument doesn't depend on an expansion of its
markets, though such expansion was without doubt eminently possible).

Compaq was stupid.  Compaq was sleazy.  Compaq was wrong.  And it's time to
fix it.

