Jeff Killeen <Jeff@IDM-IO.com> wrote:
>"Bill Todd" <billtodd@metrocast.net> wrote in message
>news:PlXM7.149549$dk.11004922@bin1.nnrp.aus1.giganews.com...
>
>> The alternative is to force no port at all:  rather, take the Alpha
>decision
>> and shove it right back down Compaq's throat.
>
>Bill all barbs aside - there is no way this is going to happen and all that
>will happen from this Jihad of yours is the potential to inflict significant
>collateral damage on OVMS.

Agreed.  Up until the beginning of the year, it would have been just
possible for Alpha to have had a long-term future, though it was getting
pretty unlikely.  Now, it isn't.  Well, it may be theoretically possible,
but there isn't a realistic chance.

The key is NOT Compaq's dithering and blithering, but the dispersal of
the Alpha team and the length of time that there has been little or
no development (on the EV8) already.  By the time that the Alpha could
be back as a leading architecture, most customers will have moved off.

The best hope for Alpha succession is that HP's shareholders will turn
down the merger, and that Compaq or its receivers will sell the Alpha
to a company that will do something positive and long-term.  But I
doubt that it would be the Alpha as such, though it could well be a
design that is compatible enough to make VMS porting straightforward.

[ To those who are not deeply into this area:  the difficulties of
porting a modern system are NOT in the instruction set, as conversion
is easy and most code is in reasonably clean high-level languages,
but in things like the exception handling and process control models
used by the ISA. ]

I am not optimistic, but there is a chance.



