I don't know the answer, but it *could* be because  a) VMS is so stable that
as long as you don't commit to doing anything new with it (and who would
given the way its owner treats it?) it requires really minimal shepherding
and/or  b) many remaining users are loath to put much money or effort into a
platform they are moving away from dependence on as fast as they can.  As
for the requirement for non-VMS experience as well, that could follow from
the second point (given an alternative, a company wouldn't choose to hire
someone without skills that would be useful for an indefinite period - even
a contractor is someone with whom a potentially on-going relationship has
more value than a known short-term one).

That's the demand side of the death-spiral.  The supply side is more
obvious:  seeing the decreased interest in VMS and funding development and
advertising less because of it, rather than recognizing that with
*increases* in development and promotion the still-lucrative market would
instead expand (and the demand-side death-spiral be reversed).

- bill





