In comp.sys.dec Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> wrote:
: More evidence of the supposed 'synergies' HP's Carly has been
: detailing to analysts seems to be emerging. In a feature on SuperDLT
: vs Ultrium in this week's "Computing" (UK) IDC and Gartner are both
: quoted as saying that DLT as originally developed by DEC and SuperDLT
: as developed by Quantum will be dead end products after the HP merger.
: Supposedly DLT/SuperDLT sales are still mainly through Compaq branded
: products or related sales. WIth HP a co-developer of Ultrium both
: Gartner and IDC predict that the new HP will phase out Compaq DLT
: products in favour of Ultrium. Both further predict that Quantum will
: not be able to keep SuperDLT alive without the backing of a major
: vendor.

: So there we have the final wreckage of yet another DEC developed
: technology. First flogged off by Palmer then nailed stone dead by
: Curly's love affair with Carly.

: I think I'll setup an IT analysis company. Then I can rake in a
: fortune by simply inviting Carly and Curly for dinner then repeating,
: for a fee of course,  whatever nonsense they whisper in my ear as "my
: predictions".
: --
: Alan

I'll believe this one when I see it.  I just came off a 3 year contract at a
major manufacturer's site in North Eastern Ohio.  We had a pretty good mix
of boxes, Compaqs, HP 9000 Unix machines, Dells, IBMs, and the only
consistent backup media format was DLT.  Never seen so many DLT drives in my
life.  And I dealt with 3 other international locations of theirs that all
specifically requested DLT drives with any new hardware.

Heck, I spec'ed out some new HP 9000 A-class hardware for them a few months
back and HP wouldn't even offer me an Ultrium drive in their "Unix" storage
lineup.  All they have for Unix & HP 3000-class machines are DATs and DLT
drives.  If this is going to happen HP's really going to have to get behind
Ultrium and push it, something they haven't done since they introduced the
format 2-3 years ago...

I just can't see it happening, myself...Especially when HP just made a bunch
of hoopla over introducing their first SuperDLT drive very recently. 
Actually, I've seen a new SuperDLT drive mechanism out there recently,
instead of the traditional 5.25" full-height form factor it's a more
reasonable half-height form factor like a DAT drive.  And I believe the
mechanism was being built by a new manufacturer other than Quantum, though 
I can't quite recall the builder's name.  I will say that having a
half-height drive available would dramatically expand the usability of the
format, since there have been several servers over the years that I've not
been able to conveniently put a DLT drive into without going to an external
drive.

Chris

