|> technology to do so) and started 'thinking strategically' that they blew it.

No, DEC had a very clear technical direction.  Also, don't confuse
listening to customers with relying on requests (especially from
executives) to make technical decisions.  One example:

The original PowerPC was planned for SCSI disks, but when
it was eventually shipped it was with primarily IDE ones.  This
was not done to save costs.

Apparently some boneheaded executives told the IBM marketdroids
that they wanted IDE because they needed to be able to reuse the
disks in their old systems in their new ones.  Many of us told
IBM that was a damn-fool idea, and almost nobody (except a few
home hackers) was reusing disks on PCs.  IBM should have ignored
the non-technical customers and stuck with their original decision.


