On Sat, 24 Nov 2001, JF Mezei wrote:

> Terry C Shannon wrote:
> > >
> > > WARNING: 'Mixed Cluster' may not mean Vax+Alpha+itanic... It was
> > > used to mean Alpha+IPF, and when asked, the Q people passed on
> > > that one...
> > >
> > 
> > That would seem to be a reasonable assumption from a CPQ financial
> > standpoint. Perhaps not so reasonable to the ~150K VAX users still out
> > there, though.
> 
> What would be so difficult in allowing VAX to cluster with an IA64 ? If the
> VMS code on IA64 is to be common with that of Alpha, and alpha supports VMS as
> a fellow cluster member, I don't see why it would be such a big deal to allow
> IA64 to support VAX cluster members.

We discussed this before.  It's not a big deal.  It just requires lots
of qualification testing, which they don't want to do.  The semi-official
answer (from Steve Hoffman, IIRC), was that they wouldn't intentionally
break anything, and if any problems were discovered, they would try to
fix them, but they weren't going to make any legally binding promises.

When you think about it, they still have to support VAX-Alpha mixed
clusters, and if they are also supporting Alpha-IPF mixed clusters,
either it has to work, or the clustering software needs to behave
differently depending on what other nodes are in the cluster.  To
make the clustering software sensitive to the other nodes would be
a fair amount of work for no conceivable benefit.

Imagine this situation:  1) Boot up a cluster consisting of a bunch
of Alpha nodes.  2) Boot a VAX into the cluster.  Works fine.  Now
imagine a second situation:  1) Boot up a cluster consisting of a
bunch of Alpha nodes.  2) Boot an IPF into the cluster.  Should also
work fine.  So how do all the Alphas at initial boot time know
which situation will prevail, and start doing all the stuff that
won't work with VAXes?

The only gotcha's I can think of are things like mixed cluster caching,
where they use the lowest common denominator.  They could make VAX'es not
recognize that IPF's should be treated like Alphas, (but why bother?) or
they could remove the Alpha's code that makes it play nice with VAXes
from the IPF version, but they have said they are aiming for common
sources with Alpha and IPF, and this would be needless conditionalization.

I think that there are bigger things to worry about.

