The below is amusing but like Todd full of unproven assumptions taken as
fact.

For the record I was a dual History and CS major and I run my own business
that is based on Digital/Compaq technology since 1970.  A business that
still that still generates significant revenue from application written for
Digital OS's.

Yours and Todd's arguments are all based on a fundamental assumption that I
don't agree with it, and apparently like Todd you believe that people who
disagree with your fundamental assumption are idiots.  The fundamental
assumption is that the market place values on a chip rather than a solution.
Even Todd at this point will acknowledge that a multi-IA64 processor could
achieve the same performance level as a multi-Alpha processor system.  I
believe the statement he made was enough low performance processors could be
made to equal the performance of a high performance processor.  His way of
phrasing it not mine.  Given that what does the debate come down to assuming
Compaq delivers on its promise that conversion will only be a recompile
away?  It comes down to economics.  Both Todd and I agree that it will
require more IA64 processors to equal the performance of given set of Alpha
processors.  So what that boils the argument down to is can Compaq deliver
equal, better, or cheaper performance with IA64.  Todd believes they
absolutely cannot -  and because of the fundamental assumption above about
the chip that the customer base will abandon Compaq in droves.  I on the
other hand believe that once Compaq makes the conversion to IA64 OVMS will
have a better future in the market because the question of the long term
viability, which has haunted Alpha since day one, will go away.  That in
fact the customer loyality is to OVMS.  Unlike Todd who won't acknowledge
the high risks with EV8 I will acknowledge that for the current strategy to
succeed it will require Compaq to execute flawlessly.

I choose not to debate Todd's opinion's as fact.  That is a fruitless and
foolish use of one's time.  They are just opinions and his opinion and my
opinion at this point in time are not fact but unproven supposition.  Anyone
who ascribes such absolutes as right or wrong to them has questionable
judgement.

At age 48 the way I have found to detect to the true idiots in this world is
to find the people who label those who disagree with them as idiots.  It
demonstrates the objective and open minded attitude they have to ideas and
perspectives other than their own.  It also means that they tend to see
their opinions as equaling fact.

The technical side of this debate is pointless for the most part - what has
driven the issues around Alpha in the marketplace haven't been technical
issues but issues of economics and business.  Digital's strategy always had
the flaw of assuming that if they built a chip so superior  the world would
beat a path to its door.  It is based on the false assumption that
businesses buy technology (e.g. processor architectures) rather than that
businesses buy solutions...

Let me wrap-up with this  - assume Compaq delivers on its promise that IA64
is only a compile away and delivers IA64 based servers running OVMS or Tru64
that is equal to or better than the competition in price performance.  Grant
the assumption for the moment.  Do you think many will care that OVMS is now
running on IA64 versus Alpha.  If it is only a  recompile away, and the
price performance is there, what rational business reason would any
unemotional person have for caring it is not running on Alpha?



