Yes, you are right, the ad is a message.  Incidently, as part of the ad, 
a rather nice piece of hardware was shown, if not promoted specifically. 
  What did we get from Compaq?  A bunch of butterflies and a truck load 
of windoz junk.  Quite a big difference, and noticed by those decision 
makers who could recognize the difference.


> I think this is very illustrative of the mistake people make thinking all
> that Compaq needs to do is advertise OVMS.  Note these comments are being
> made in the context of OVMS expanding its market.  Compaq needs to create an
> identity with CIO's that it equals best in class enterprise solutions.  It
> appears that Compaq is just about to achieve that goal with NSK and the
> application that kicked it over the edge seems to be ZLE.  They could market
> the NSK technology to the general business community until the cows came and
> they would achieve nothing except in a few specialized sectors.  When they
> started marketing ZLE that was a business concept that CIO's and CEO's could
> understand.  They have had some big wins as result, all of which are not
> public yet.  But is was the solution and not the technology that brought
> Compaq into this new market for NSK.  Just advertising OVMS won't
> significantly expand its market.  It will require some solution which shows
> OVMS is clearly better suited for that has perceived business value.


Again, you just sound too much like a shill for the Compaq windoz crowd. 
  By not mentioning VMS, how many current VMS people who were on the 
fence decided that VMS was indeed toast, and they'd do well to move away 
from VMS as fast as they can.

I've mentioned it before, and will again because it's so indicative of 
the problem.  I have a customer with a VMS based application.  Much of 
the office activity at this customer's site is indeed office automation, 
were windoz is rather helpful.  But they do have some serious, and 
financial, applications.  They will, without fail, tell you that the VMS 
based applications are the most robust and reliable applications that 
they are running.  In the next breath they'll tell you that they will 
not implement another application on VMS.  Why?  Two reasons.  Windoz 
has satisfied a large part of their business quite well, and they cite 
all the worthless stories about people already knowing windows and there 
is such a shorter training effort required.  But dig a bit, and they 
admit that they are afraid that VMS has a limited life.  The first 
argument is bogus.  Training new employees is dependant upon the 
application, not the user interface.  The real reason they will not add 
to the VMS based applications is the fear that VMS is going away.

Now, where did that fear come from.  DEC, and then Compaq.  That's 
where.  Even just mentioning VMS in advertising, without VMS specific 
advertising, would reach the right ears, and many current VMS users 
would re-think their beliefs about the future of VMS.  Compaq has done 
the opposite, and cultivated the current fears.

Don't try to tell me that Compaq wants the VMS business, on VMS.  If you 
do, I will bluntly call you a lier and an idiot.


> It is very hard for individuals who find OVMS as valuable in and of itself
> to understand that for expanding into markets its is applications
> (solutions) corporate managers are looking for. These managers need to
> believe that OVMS offers something unique that makes that application much
> better suited to be deployed on OVMS.  A pre-condition of that is either
> Compaq or an ISV developing those killer applications.
> 
> Here is a simple test if you want to expand OVMS's market.  Write an ad that
> would appeal the CEO of the company who knows little about computers.  Ads
> that appeal to fear only tend to work if the person first has that fear and
> second believes that the fear can be mitigated.  Just saying your system
> will crash less won't appeal to someone who believes the systems stay up
> enough now or that every computer is going to crash.  Ads that appeal to
> gaining advantage are most effective e.g. putting your business on the
> Internet will double your income.  A good example of the gaining advantage
> type ad is the IBM ad where buyers are in a mom and pop shop and the shop
> owners check their handheld and point out they "have 10,000 units in the
> warehouse in New Jersey".
> 
> Whether one likes it or not CIO's and CEO's tend not to be swayed by the
> fine points in the differences between OS technologies.  They tend to be
> driven by applications they find highly valuable...


While I will agree with positive messages, I will point out that CEOs 
are not who will make such decisions.  Any good CEOs will rely on the 
advice of their IT experts.  The message to the IT experts for some time 
is that Compaq will not help VMS in any manner.  Gee, wonder where they 
got that idea.  Also wonder what it would take to change that 
understanding.  First, stop sending the 'wrong' message, and then send 
the 'right' message.  Wouldn't need to be much, for those who would 
perfer to remain on VMS.

Same for the ISVs.  They didn't desert VMS.  They feel that DEC and 
Compaq have deserted them.


