This can be explained easily. We run SAP on Compaq Wintel hardware in
parts of our Scandinavian operations - Compaq recommended Wintel about
three years ago. We have not been especially satisfied with the
performance of the systems so about 6 months ago we called in Compaq
to discuss switching to ES40/ES45s running SAP under Tru64. Without us
ever asking about WIntel Compaq first produced a sample configuration
using Tru64 *then* produced a sample config using Compaq Wintel. For
anything other than especially large SAP configs the Compaq
configuration tools always produce a lower cost solution with the
Wintel option than the Alpha/Tru64 system. Compaq sales are not
allowed to suggest that the Tru64 system will be any more reliable
than Wintel - although depending on the salesman and the customer they
might whisper this in private. The result is the customer is nudged
towards a low profit margin (for Compaq) Wintel system and away from a
high margin Alpha solution. I consider this gross corporate insanity.

So we called in Compaq to discuss buying Alphas with Tru64 (having
failed to persuade Compaq that there is a market for SAP on VMS) and
they attempted to persuade us Wintel was just as good and cheaper.
Then along came the Alpha bombshell followed by the Compaq/HP merger
and we are now likely to buy Sun. That's the reality of Compaq's
madness and that's why 50% of Compaq SAP wins are Wintel. We were sold
a Compaq Wintel solution (I was not involved in that decision but am
involved in replacement discussions) then tried to upgrade to Alpha
and were again quoted WIntel. This is Compaq company policy.

If you call in Sun they will attempt to sell their own systems - not
Microsoft's. No wonder Sun has the majority of SAP installations. To
the best of my knowledge HP do push SAP on HP-UX but I am not
confident that this will continue after listening to Carly Fiorina.
Compaq are currently virtually alone in aggressively pushing SAP on
WIntel.

