THAT IS ABSOLUTELY _NOT_ MY POSITION!  I believe OVMS can and should be
saved.  I also believe the way to fast track OVMS to a early and unwarranted
death is for Compaq to expend the resources available to try to position
OVMS as something the market will no longer accept.  Specifically I do not
believe that the market will accept OVMS as a general purpose OS.

I strongly believe that what harmed OVMS the most was strategy decisions
Digital made as opposed to marketing decisions.  Specially they never
positioned OVMS in niche markets.  As a result OVMS was positioned as a
"jack of all trades" and what usually comes along with that is a lack of
identity which means the market perceives that lack of identity as equaling
"master of none".  They created a value proposition for OVMS that was based
solely on it being the universal platform which feel apart when Unix and
then Windows achieved far greater acceptance.  Then what was even more
tragic was calling it OpenVMS.  In the brand name it reminded everyone of
the issue.  It would be like Firestone today renaming its tires SafetyATX.

I believe Compaq needs to pursue the same strategy for OVMS it has for NSK.
Find applications that have strong business appeal.  Applications that are
perceived as far better to be run on OVMS.  That will gain acceptance for
OVMS.  It is the applications, and a perceived special value to running them
on OVMS, that will bring OVMS back.

I also believe that people who are pushing to Compaq to position OVMS as a
universal OS are doing more harm than good to OVMS.  In addition they are
seeking something for OVMS no other OS has achieved in the industry.
Neither Unix or Windows is perceived as being viable as a universal
platform.  I know Digital originally positioned OVMS as from the desktop the
datacenter but it turns out that is something no OS may ever achieve because
of design compromises that must be made.

Bottom line - to save OVMS Compaq must create "a master of certain forms of
computing" image for it and must do so by proof points achieved through
highly desirable business applications...






