wrote:


>  For the OS
>that made clustering real in the marketplace it amazes me how people dismiss
>today the concept of  multiple servers working together to achieve the
>needed horsepower required to equal a work load.  It sounds so much like the
>IBM guys did in the early 1980's.

Because it doesn't work properly with NT., Can't you get that through
your head. By the time you have the 50 or so NT servers up and running
necessary to handle the workload with redundancy you are probably at a
crash frequency of anything between 1-10 crashes per week up to 1-10
crashes per day. You need multiple backup DNS servers, multiple mail
servers, multiple domain controllers, multiple servers, multiple hot
standbys and multiple sysadmins. And even when you are incredibly
careful you spend all your time patching systems and chasing
mysterious bugs, Thus our NT mantra "Shut up and reboot" (from a
Dilbert cartoon). In the past you could run all this on one big VMS
box and might see a VMS crash less than once a year. Just try running
everything on one big NT box and see how long it stays up in real use.

Then despite the heroic efforts of our NT team occasional viruses
still make their way on site causing incredible amounts of wasted
time. And I could go on and on...

Yes I agree you *can* configure a reasonably reliable NT based server
farm for *particular apps* but you start trying to put similar mixes
of different packages on the same server as you could with VMS or even
Unix and you are in for a shock. Take Windows Terminal Server
(multi-user NT). We have a 4 processor Compaq box with 5 600Mhz
Pentium processors which supports remote (dialup, WAN etc) login to
the Windows desktop for up to 30 users. It barely stays up 24hrs
without a mysterious crash and we force a reboot every night to help
things along. VMS was never this bad and even Unix left this stage
behind years and years ago.

All our systems are kept up to latest MS patch level btw.

So you go to virtually any site running a mixture of Unix, VMS and NT
and 99% will say that the NT systems create the most headaches. NT
deserves only to replace the Win9x codebase. It is nowhere near
Enterprise Ready. Windows is not up to it. You and Compaq should
listen to the customers who tell you this and "get over it"..

