Well, since we're examining all possibilities here, do you have any
knowledge that Microsoft would refuse to resurrect Win64 on Alpha if Compaq
footed the bill?  Sure, it would make the Compaq leadership that let it get
axed look bad, but they already look bad enough to get summarily fired so a
little more wouldn't matter.

Win64 was reportedly already running in field test 2+ years ago on Alpha as
64-bit Win2K (A.K.A. NT 5.0), and continued in development internally on
Alpha at least until Itanic platforms appeared.  Given that Windows XP is
A.K.A. NT 5.1, the work required to get 64-bit XP running on Alpha should be
trivial, at least compared with that required to port Windows to any other
platform.

And the Achilles' heel of not being able to run IA32 binaries at native
speed disappears in any comparison with IA64, since by all appearances IA32
code running on Alpha under FX!32 would significantly out-perform the same
IA32 code running on IA64.

I'll take a wild guess that getting XP running on Alpha (now that the 64-bit
Itanic version is supposedly complete, presumably including Microsoft
applications) would cost a lot less than porting VMS to Itanic.  Not that XP
has yet shown itself to be anything like a 'must have' for an architecture
to succeed in Alpha's market segments, but if/when it does this option
eliminates the problem you suggested above.

