...

> All that Compaq has ever said technically is...
>
> 1) The performance gap between EV8 and its competitors would be closing

No:  it stated specifically that the performance gap between Alpha and
*IA64* would be closing, which is a far more difficult assertion to support
(since the opposite appears more likely).

>
> 2) That EV8 would require flawless execution to succeed

If you examine that statement for content, it's by definition false:  the
advantages offered by EV8 were substantial, and would clearly have given it
the ability to weather at least minor delays.

...

> If
> one does think in business terms what Compaq did looks like a classic
> business decision that was based on the _reward_ from EV8 not being worth
> _return_ given the required _investment_ to make it happen.

Part of the reward for EV8 would have been avoidance of the significant
decline in Alpha sales that now appears to be occurring.  There's reason to
suspect that merely continued sales at existing levels (let alone increases
that were almost certainly possible with even minimal effort) would have
made completing EV8 development at least marginally justifiable financially
*even if EV8 then never shipped*.

What Compaq did looks much more like a classic business blunder arrived at
by incompetents with no understanding of the underlying market who did a
half-assed job of cobbling up numbers (and other equally dubious assertions)
to rationalize it.  If they don't like that appearance, then it's their job
to come up with a far more convincing explanation than anything they've
offered so far.

