> values of N, because processor prices (assuming that Itanic's ever *do*
come
> down) just don't contribute much to the cost of a mid-range or larger
> server.

Well well well - at long last Todd acknowledges that the IA64 could be
configured to provide the same performance as Alpha.

Folks more than anything else the above argument from Todd should make you
question his fundamental understanding of this subject...

1) What drives the costs of this class of server is NOT the actual chips
used in the box but the R&D costs.  The actual cost to manufacture the
server is a fraction of what they sell for.

2) If you look at the number of N-Processor Alpha servers Compaq sells and
compare that to the number of N-Processor Proliant boxes Compaq sells you
quickly realize to potential spread R&D costs over a much larger base - a
larger base than most, it not all, of its competitors.

3) Compaq is moving to QuickBlade servers with InfiniBand fabric. The design
is very much optimized to allow the easy addition of CPU power.  You should
be able to get a NDA presentation from your salesperson on the first
generation of QuickBlade servers.  (BTW Compaq sells more dense rack servers
than HP, Dell, and Sun combined today).  The QuickBlade servers today are
only the first step.

4) Compaq is going to have to sink the R&D dollars into developing high
performance N-Processor IA64 anyway.  It was not a choice between Compaq
spending the dollars designing Alpha or IA64 servers.  It was a choice
between IA64 and Alpha or just IA64.  The money saved in R&D costs can be
passed on by Compaq to make the additional server blades you will need no
more expensive than the Alpha systems.

Even if we accept Todd's contention that it will take two IA64 processors to
equal one Alpha processor the cost won't be any higher because the R&D costs
will be spread over a much larger base.

Folks mid to high level server pricing is NOT a function of you add up the
cost of the chips, then decide your profit, and slap a price on the system.
There use to be VAX systems that the actual cost to manufacture them was 8
percent of what they sold for.  Todd in his own perverse way has final
focused the discussion on R&D costs versus manufacturing costs were it
should have been all along.

