news:V2eM7.94807$qx2.6012891@bin5.nnrp.aus1.giganews.com...
> As I indicated elsewhere, I don't know any details about it, though
'blades'
> have been mentioned enough that one can infer some things about their
> character.  As I also said elsewhere, I'd welcome information about where
> Compaq's particular 'blade' approach is documented and would be happy to
> comment in more detail after I've read it.

There is a QuickBlade NDA's available for Compaq customers.  Being NDA
material one must be cautious about how far one can go. I would suggest
anyone who is interested in this get the NDA presentation.

> The comments I made above, however, are generic to all multi-processor
> systems (even SMP, though ccNUMA seems to be getting hard to avoid these
> days and I suspect is at least as much a part of Compaq blades as it is of
> the current Wildfire and future Marvel products).

The QuickBlade strategy is based on high density server packaging which has
different groups and types of servers for different workloads all in the
same rack.  It is based on resource provisioning and dynamic resource
allocation.  It is geared towards the typical commercial environment where
many different types of jobs need to be handled at once.  It is designed to
be have the different groups of servers built into a high density single
rack.  Initially there could be many blades in a rack with many more
processors (do not read that as _all_ processors being tightly coupled
together).  It is a highly managed set of computing resources in rack with
shared storage.  The first family is due soon but the ultimate design that
we be the follow-on for Alpha customers will come later.  Pay close
attention to the code names "wind", "fire", and "ice"...





