Typically clueless analogy on your part.  Comparing clusters with mainframes
is comparing apples and oranges, whereas comparing large-scale MPs is
comparing apples and apples:  when you're using identical approaches (so
identical that the same framework supports both Alphas and Itanics, in this
case), cost-effectiveness (rather than architectural approach) becomes the
differentiator.

Your entire thesis rests on the assumption that volume will drive down
Itanic-based server prices dramatically.  But mid-range-and-up servers will
*never* have that kind of volume, and what volume they do have will be
divided among Itanic and the current players that make Itanic look pretty
sickly.

You've agreed elsewhere with what I've been saying all along - that the cost
of the processors in such servers doesn't make much difference to their
price.  And the Itanic processors are the *only* components in those servers
that will enjoy the kind of volume that drives prices down (assuming for the
moment that people find them more attractive in low-end platforms than they
seem likely to):  the entry price of the blade framework itself means that
you won't spring for it unless you're pretty sure that your needs will start
or grow beyond the growth capacity of the low-end non-blade MP servers that
it competes with in the low end.

In other words, an attempt to create a seamless growth path from low-end on
up without replacing hardware always runs up against the problem that
purpose-built low-end systems are *always* more cost-effective (over the
range of applications that they satisfy) then expandible systems that
require at least portions of that expandible framework (with its added
burden of both R&D and actual parts costs) right down at the entry level.
And the effective range of those purpose-built low-end servers keeps
increasing.  Yes, that means the high end keeps getting squeezed, and
perhaps eventually it will get pretty much completely squeezed out.  But
until that time comes, the high end servers will be differentiated by
exactly those characteristics that can't be incorporated in low-end servers
without significantly increasing their price - and the price difference will
often be more than sufficient to justify starting with purpose-built low-end
servers with the idea that a more expandible platform can simply be swapped
in if/when it proves necessary.

So the volume/price argument for blades just doesn't fly, since low-end
servers will price blades out of that high-volume, low-end market.  And
without the volume/price argument, the feasibility of just using twice as
many IA64 blades to make up for its mediocre performance doesn't fly either
(even for applications that easily admit to such partitioning).  Not that I
expect you to understand such logic (since I've already presented it in a
bit less detail before), but I suspect others will.

