> still false but the thought process is revealed...

Ok, let's cut through the bullshit.


Compaq desired DECs service business.  Why?  The concept of steady 
income without having to sell x amount of hardware can be enticing.

Having acquired that service business, Compaq has trashed it.  Why? 
Because DECs service business centered around it's enterprise customers. 
  Compaq wanted the same type of revenue from it's PC customers.  Well 
guess what Jack, it ain't never gonna happen.  In not really 
understanding what DEC's service business was all about Compaq has 
totally screwed up it's reasons for buying DEC.  Then there is the joke 
of calling their wintel stuff 'enterprise systems', as if that would 
cause customers to suddenly buy service for the re-badged PCs.

Then there was all the baggage that came with DEC.  Non-wintel 
enterprise systems.  Compaq never wanted them, and had VMS slated for 
the scrap heap until Rich Marcello pulled a good one on them.  He 
promised something Compaq never thought he could do, and extracted a 
promise from them to keep VMS if he could succeed.  Well, he set some 
small goals, and met them.  This saved VMS, for a while anyway.

Take a look at what Compaq has done to the substantial funds DEC spent 
on R&D.  That alone will plainly show you that Compaq isn't interested 
in being a technology leader.  All they want to do is assemble boxes. 
They want someone else to spend the R&D bucks.  They want to be another 
Dell.

Compaq's big mistake was buying DEC.  There was nothing there that they 
really wanted.  Their understanding of the service business was flawed, 
so even that wasn't anything that they wanted.

So, you can rationalize all you want.  Compaq wants to be a box 
assembler, and they're dumping anything that doesn't fit within that 
scope.  Look at it from their perspective.  If all that's available to 
everyone is Intel, then they won't look any worse than anyone else.  Why 
should they make any more effort than that?

In the end, there will be IBM and Dell.  The only issues are how 
everyone will arrive at that point.  DEC could have been there also. 
They would have had to carefully analyze what they could do, and not do, 
and make sure they didn't blow money on things that would be losers. 
Palmer took the easy way out.  Easy for him, rather rough for the rest 
of us.

Dave


