"David Froble" <davef@tsoft-inc.com> wrote in message
news:3C01F412.60205@tsoft-inc.com...
> Compaq desired DECs service business.  Why?  The concept of steady
> income without having to sell x amount of hardware can be enticing.
>
> Having acquired that service business, Compaq has trashed it.  Why?
> Because DECs service business centered around it's enterprise customers.
> Compaq wanted the same type of revenue from it's PC customers.  Well
> guess what Jack, it ain't never gonna happen.  In not really
> understanding what DEC's service business was all about Compaq has
> totally screwed up it's reasons for buying DEC.

If Compaq had done their homework they would have never bought Digital.  You
are correct that they bought Digital for the services group.  What Compaq
learned later was over 2/3's of that revenue came from servicing the
installed base (read maintenance contracts).  What they were looking for was
a IT consulting services group - similar to an EDS type of operation.  They
found that group inside of Digital services unit that did true IT consulting
services was much smaller than they thought.

This I know for absolute fact - Compaq did not want the services unit
thinking it would create a revenue as you suggest above.  Why they wanted it
was based on the theory that if Compaq as hired as your IT consultant than
naturally from that you would perceive Compaq products as having a value add
and the projects you engaged Compaq for would all be deployed with Compaq
products.  It was very much trying to create a value add market
differentiator.

The problem Compaq has today, and I suspect the problem HP has today, is
that their true IT consulting services group does not have critical mass.
Both HP and Compaq believe they are in a very bad spot.  They are neither
efficient box marker like Dell nor do they have the critical mass to be an
IT consulting company like IBM.  The business realities of competing with
Dell, where the value add is in manufacturing and delivery, would require
profound and painful changes.  The business realities of competing with IBM
are far more friendly to keeping most of the current company intact but it
requires a much larger IT consulting practice than either HP of Compaq has
today.

> Then there is the joke
> of calling their wintel stuff 'enterprise systems', as if that would
> cause customers to suddenly buy service for the re-badged PCs.

For the record I don't believe WinTel stuff is enterprise class as we define
enterprise class but there are troubling data points around.  For example
Compaq has a lot of SAP deployments - the majority of those deployments are
on WinTel boxes.  When you say to them WinTel doesn't do enterprise they
point back to the customer demand they are getting as say really.  Note
their customers, not Compaq, made this choice.  The point being while we
know WinTel in the datacenter isn't there many customers apparently don't
believe this.

I hinted at this in another posting.  But I hate to say it but where people
say WinTel is today, and the things said about its short comings, very much
remind me of what the IBM folks said about deploying enterprise applications
on OVMS in the early 1980's.

> Then there was all the baggage that came with DEC.  Non-wintel
> enterprise systems.  Compaq never wanted them, and had VMS slated for
> the scrap heap until Rich Marcello pulled a good one on them.  He
> promised something Compaq never thought he could do, and extracted a
> promise from them to keep VMS if he could succeed.  Well, he set some
> small goals, and met them.  This saved VMS, for a while anyway.

Compaq wants the OVMS and Tru64 customers and applications.  IMO the main
reason why OVMS is safe that Compaq highly values this customer base.  Lets
keep this in the context of prior to the HP merger - I fully agree with you
that if Compaq could have waived its magic wand and converted all the OVMS
customers to Tru64 they would have done it in a heartbeat (Tru64 and not
Wintel - the wet dream of senior Compaq management was for Tru64 to achieve
Sun status).  They value the customers but not the operating system.  The
problem is they have never been able to solve how to keep those customers if
they attempt to move them to any other platform.  Lets face it - Compaq
wants to be an enterprise company and the only significant foothold they
have there is OVMS.  Tru64 and NSK just do not have the numbers to be
significant.

> Take a look at what Compaq has done to the substantial funds DEC spent
> on R&D.  That alone will plainly show you that Compaq isn't interested
> in being a technology leader.  All they want to do is assemble boxes.
> They want someone else to spend the R&D bucks.  They want to be another
> Dell.

There we strongly disagree.  Compaq understands what it would require to go
head to head with Dell.  If they ever make that decision you will see a cost
reduction program unlike anything you ever convinced of before.  If Compaq
decides to compete with Dell head on within 18 months Compaq will spin-off
(one way or another) everything but the WinTel Stuff, storage, and some of
services.  The rest of the stuff would have to go to create the cost
structure where Compaq could compete with Dell.

The problem is Compaq does NOT want to be Dell but they have yet to find a
way to achieve the critical mass to compete with IBM.  Unless they get out
of this state of being neither as efficient at moving iron as Dell and not
being larger enough to have IT consulting services that are credible then
Compaq is a dead duck.

> Compaq's big mistake was buying DEC.  There was nothing there that they
> really wanted.  Their understanding of the service business was flawed,
> so even that wasn't anything that they wanted.

I would agree with you that Compaq did not get what they expected out of
either the purchase of Tandem or Digital.  I strongly disagree with you as
to what their motivation was. Their motivation remains to create a value add
beyond being who can deliver the fastest and cheapest WinTel box.  It is
counter-cultural to what Compaq is about.

Compaq bought Tandem and Digital simply because they were what was available
and not because they were idea.

> So, you can rationalize all you want.  Compaq wants to be a box
> assembler, and they're dumping anything that doesn't fit within that
> scope.  Look at it from their perspective.  If all that's available to
> everyone is Intel, then they won't look any worse than anyone else.  Why
> should they make any more effort than that?

I believe you will find that in their heart of hearts all of Compaq's senior
management and their board of directors believe that attempting to compete
with Dell as a box assembler is a death sentence.  That is Compaq if can't
find a value add beyond building and shipping the fastest box that they are
not viable as a corporation.

Do NOT equate poor execution with a lack of desire.  The desire is there but
they yet have found a way to execute successfully.

> In the end, there will be IBM and Dell.  The only issues are how
> everyone will arrive at that point.  DEC could have been there also.
> They would have had to carefully analyze what they could do, and not do,
> and make sure they didn't blow money on things that would be losers.
> Palmer took the easy way out.  Easy for him, rather rough for the rest
> of us.

Compaq and HP are betting that there is a 3rd alternative which is IBM like
IT services but based on industry standard architectures.  If HP and Compaq
cannot create this 3rd alternative then I agree with you that in the end
there will only be Dell and IBM.  IBM when your value proposition is based
on industrial strength deployment.  Dell when your value proposition is
based on fastest and cheapest delivery.  Both HP and Compaq will become a
shadow of the companies they are today.

David I do not believe Compaq where it sits today has a viable overall
business strategy.  History has shown in all industries that companies that
are either perceived as providing the best premium service or the best
commodity product are the ones that make the money (e.g. Daimler and
Toyota).  That has been proven over and over again.  Compaq has put itself
in a situation where can't provide the best commodity product or the best
premium service.  Companies that find themselves in that half way always run
into trouble as the market matures.  (Note I said premium service and not
just premium product)

Also David I think you are reading flawed execution on Compaq's part as
being what their true desire is.  Believe me working with Encompass I have
spent too much time with them for their execs to be putting on that good of
a snow job. Their comments are too repetitive and too pervasive.  They
really don't want to get into the Dell business and they really do want
their value add to be being able to build your IT infrastructure.





