Unfortunately, when one is dealing with an axe murderer, discussing the fine
points of sharpening the axe isn't really one's first priority.

If people can find the sense of purpose to create a unified force that
Compaq actually *has to* listen to (under threat of being forced out of
business otherwise), rather than just wring their hands and complain, then
some day we may return to a situation where discussing finer technical
points makes sense.  If not, then any effort expended is likely going to be
wasted effort, since it won't begin to address the fundamental issues that
seem likely to make VMS either stagnate or disappear altogether.

The problem is simple:  existing Compaq management don't see VMS as anything
but a pain in the ass that generates sufficient revenue that they're
grudgingly willing to put up with it.  Lacking change in that attitude,
nothing substantive will change with VMS.  Change in that attitude appears
impossible (without changing the management itself):  we've certainly tried
hard enough and failed.  And given the level of duplicity that this
management has demonstrated time and time again, further discussion with
them - even if they were to claim to welcome it - may well be wasted effort
too.

What's so hard for people to understand about this picture?  Or is it just
so uncomfortable to accept it that it's easier to hope for the best, even
though that has been a consistently losing strategy too many times to count?

