|> 
|> IBM should never be underestimated. Remember the US trade rag Datamation? It
|> was sometime in the first half of the nineties that Datamation proclaimed
|> the death of the mainframe.

Back in the late 1970s, many of us predicted the imminent death
of the mainframe - as a general-purpose interaction server and
mainstay of research computing.  We were right.  IBM didn't take
notice, and lost that market, more or less completely.

In the 1980s, many of us (inside and outside IBM) said that IBM
had lost its way, and was being sidelined.  That was also true.
Somehow, and I have never worked out how, IBM has managed to
regain its position without making its direction clear.

No, do not underestimate IBM.  My guess at the secret is that the
company doesn't forget that the 'B' stands for Business.


