|> 
|> Samsung was approached back in July about continuing Alpha development, but
|> apparently opted not to do so. It's an expensive undertaking.
 
Hmm.  I suspect that things are not as simple as mere cost.

When API was set up, it said that it was 70% owned by Samsung, yet
it seems never to have been allowed to lead Compaq on ANY front.
My suspicion is that it was set up to allow Samsung to sell into
the DoD and NSA, therefore had to be controlled by Compaq, Compaq
didn't play fair, and Samsung got right royally shafted.

By July, Compaq had already sold off a lot of the Alpha staff.
Samsung might well have been interested in taking over the Alpha
as a going concern, but that had been made impossible by then.  In
any case, the time for that sale was years ago, but I suspect that
the DoD and NSA forbade it at that stage.

If Samsung were to have taken over the complete EV8 rights, but
only (say) 30% of the Alpha development staff, it would be at
least 2005, quite likely later, before they were in production. 
And, since Compaq would have been winding down the Alpha line
for 3-4 years before then, there would be no Alpha community and
applications base to build on.

Assuming that Samsung were shafted by Compaq over API, which I
think was the case, would they really want to give Compaq the
chance to do it again?


