John McLean wrote:
> Compaq doesn't give a rat's about the VMS or Tru64 customers or their
> applications.

Yes they do. They care to keep them there until they can find a way to move
them to NT without losing them. The revenus generated by VMS subsidize their
wintel crap business so they can't part with VMS revenus just as yet.

> extent those markets.  No advertising to the masses.  Marketing only to
> the existing customer base.

Exactly. they only care about the installed base because that is their source
of revenus that allows them to work on fixing up their core business: wintel.
They are not interested in growing VMS, they are interested in gradually
phasing out while keeping the customers.

> Compaq doesn't have a clue about what it requires to go head-to-head
> with Dell but despite this they continue to try to.

I am not so certain about this. The biggest difference between the two is
distribution. Dell uses FedEx (or whetever) with the only inventory being
parts needed to build the boxes. Compaq has a complex relationship with
distributors, resellers and retailers, each with their inefficiencies and
profit margins.

Compaq is stuck with a very archaic distribution paradigm. On the one hand, it
allows the showcasing of its products (such as Radio Shack stores), but on the
other hand, it means that their machines are more expensive.

The problem is that transitioning from the old distributor model to the newer
direct sales model is a big leap. Your existing retailers will desert you very
fast, you will lose the percent of customers who really want to see your
products before they buy. 

The other issue is that of worldwide sales. Dell is very focused on north
america now. But when you sell around the globe, you need to develop/change
your paradigm many times for each geography. Compaq has to deal with this
issue. It can't expect someone in France to call up some 800 number in houston
to order their PC and expect it delivered the next day.

I don't know how well Sony is doing, but I think that this has some very
interesting potential since they have already a low cost distribution network
for all of their gear to all sorts of electronics stores. Adding one more set
of devices (computers) will make use of the existing infrastructure.

Compaq needs about the same infrastructure to distribute a much lower numbr of machines.

Surely Comapq could easily streamline its manufacturing and parts purchased to
have that portion of the business being very competitive with Dell.

> Au contraire.  The best thing they could do would be to slash the PC
> division to ribbons.  It returns 2% of profit so it deserves to be 2% of
> the emphasis of the company.

Yes, that is the logical thing to do based on today's environment. But if your
main advisor (Winkler) tells you that wintel will rule the enterprise in a few
years, it then follows that you should focus on preparing your wintel business
to reap that market which will materialize sometime in the near future. And
when Winkler tells you that it will be a high margin business, then it becomes
less important to find a way to reduce the number of screws in each machine by
2 to save on costs.


