"Bill Todd" <billtodd@metrocast.net> wrote in message
news:DRyM7.129497$dk.9871668@bin1.nnrp.aus1.giganews.com...
>
> "Jeff Killeen" <Jeff@IDM-IO.com> wrote in message
> > To create brand identity through mass marketing for a sub-brand costs
> > hundreds of millions of dollars.  There is only mass advertising for the
> > most part for the corporate brand these days.  IBM doesn't advertise
> > AS/400's or z/390 systems.
>
> Incorrect.  I've seen several times recently a television ad in which an
> executive calls in security to investigate the disappearance of a room
full
> of servers only to be told that they have been consolidated into a single
> multi-instance Linux (clearly a z-Series) box.

Bill I am afraid you don't understand what brand advertising is.  I have
seen that ad and it is a message ad and _not_ a sub-brand identity ad.  That
ad was designed to implant in the viewers mind IBM has a solution that can
replace a room full for servers with a single box running Linux - and not to
implant the identity of z-Series sub brand.  It is very consist with their
corporate branding program which is to get you to think IBM = Solutions.
The target market for those ads (CEO's or CIO's) who would find product
messages noise that would obscure the message IBM = Solutions to your
business problems.

I think this is very illustrative of the mistake people make thinking all
that Compaq needs to do is advertise OVMS.  Note these comments are being
made in the context of OVMS expanding its market.  Compaq needs to create an
identity with CIO's that it equals best in class enterprise solutions.  It
appears that Compaq is just about to achieve that goal with NSK and the
application that kicked it over the edge seems to be ZLE.  They could market
the NSK technology to the general business community until the cows came and
they would achieve nothing except in a few specialized sectors.  When they
started marketing ZLE that was a business concept that CIO's and CEO's could
understand.  They have had some big wins as result, all of which are not
public yet.  But is was the solution and not the technology that brought
Compaq into this new market for NSK.  Just advertising OVMS won't
significantly expand its market.  It will require some solution which shows
OVMS is clearly better suited for that has perceived business value.

It is very hard for individuals who find OVMS as valuable in and of itself
to understand that for expanding into markets its is applications
(solutions) corporate managers are looking for. These managers need to
believe that OVMS offers something unique that makes that application much
better suited to be deployed on OVMS.  A pre-condition of that is either
Compaq or an ISV developing those killer applications.

Here is a simple test if you want to expand OVMS's market.  Write an ad that
would appeal the CEO of the company who knows little about computers.  Ads
that appeal to fear only tend to work if the person first has that fear and
second believes that the fear can be mitigated.  Just saying your system
will crash less won't appeal to someone who believes the systems stay up
enough now or that every computer is going to crash.  Ads that appeal to
gaining advantage are most effective e.g. putting your business on the
Internet will double your income.  A good example of the gaining advantage
type ad is the IBM ad where buyers are in a mom and pop shop and the shop
owners check their handheld and point out they "have 10,000 units in the
warehouse in New Jersey".

Whether one likes it or not CIO's and CEO's tend not to be swayed by the
fine points in the differences between OS technologies.  They tend to be
driven by applications they find highly valuable...






