I guess we'll see how it shakes out when the factors of durability and
long term storage are fully analyzed.

I see where the cartridge has some kind of local, NVRAM. One point of
contention I can see is whether the tape is still usable if the NVRAM is
destroyed.

Price points will be another issue. At $100+ (US) per cartridge, though
that has come down a bit, DLT-III and later DLT-IV were never
economical. I've seen my share of sites where scrimping on the tape
cartridge budget has led to a BACKUP scenario that produces tapes
useless for disaster recovery, and barely usable for any recovery. If
Ultrium cart.'s don't come in at under $50 US, I would think that
acceptance will come in below expectations.

'Twill be interesting. We've been waiting for something substantial to
happen in tape capacity and transfer rates as disc capacity and SAN
transfer rates have expanded much faster than those same figures for
magtape.

