Bill Todd <billtodd@metrocast.net> wrote in message
news:J0HL7.50743$8q.8009096@bin2.nnrp.aus1.giganews.com...

...

> When I looked at the new survey a while ago, I decided to give it a pass
> because several significant questions were not reasonably answerable given
> the way they were worded (a problem I also had with the original survey,
> though IIRC not to as great an extent).  Would you consider posting your
> next survey questions for suggested rewording *before* creating a final
> version for gathering responses?

(Sorry - I really should have included the details of my problems with the
current survey:)

Question 3 - the word 'confident'.  I'm certainly more confident now that I
*understand* Compaq's decision today than I was in June.  And more confident
that I understand its possible repercussions as well.  I'm also more
confident that it was a major mistake, with much solider reasons (though my
initial reaction hasn't changed).

I suspect you may have meant to ask whether people felt 'better' about the
decision, or possibly whether even if they deplored it personally they felt
it was at least reasonably defensible and/or potentially good for the
company - but even then, the question does not leave room for the
possibility that people might feel even *more* angry/disgusted/whatever
today than they felt in June.

Question 4 - what's a 'reasonable amount of time' for the port?  From
Compaq's viewpoint, instantaneously:  any delay in having an alternative
platform to sell after the point where the existing platform is declared
terminally ill is not reasonable in any reasonable sense of the term, since
this lack forces any purchases made during that delay interval to be
dependent on having faith that the new platform (or the old platform, now
that it has been officially defined as being a cash cow with minimal further
development) will indeed be satisfactory.

You could ask whether people feel that Compaq *can* complete the port in the
time that has been specified (I do).  You could then ask whether people feel
confident that Compaq *will* complete the port (I don't, but neither do I
feel strongly that it won't:  could go either way).  You could then ask
whether the time-frame that has been specified for the port is satisfactory.

Question 5 is one that few people may feel they have sufficient information
to answer.  But they likely know where *they personally* might jump, so
asking that might be better (as would asking about the likelihood of such
defection and of its extent, since many defections will likely be only
partial, at least initially).  You could also ask whom they might advise a
friend to jump to should they be asked, though again that would depend at
least somewhat on the friend's application.

Question 6.  Of course some ISVs will port their apps.  And of course some
ISVs won't port their apps.  The issue is how many fall into each camp, and
how important the "won't ports" are (which, again, will vary with each
customer).

Question 14 is the only other substantive question.  While most people may
well be able to find a response that suits them, it highlights a traditional
multiple-choice problem of asking a question and then providing an
incomplete answer space.  One way of checking completeness is to make the
answers symmetric in all dimensions - e.g., if one can opine that the merger
is good/neutral/poor for Compaq and HP individually, then 9 answer chioces
are required to cover the ground (assuming that 'neutral' also covers a 'not
sure' answer).

- bill





