Jeff Killeen wrote:
> performance.  It was Fenwick's team that came to the conclusion that the
> Alpha based follow-on to the Wildfire wouldn't offer a sufficient
> performance advantage over the IA64 servers that Compaq is also developing.

Taken by itself, this looks convincing. However, what is not being said is
whether this comparison was made of a competing Alpha versus a competing Intel
IA64, or whether they looked at what Intel MIGHT do with IA64 if all of the
Alpha intellectual property were given away and implemented in IA64.

Secondly, while VMS may be relegated to only customers who run Wildfires
(large shops), Alpha could have been much more than Wildfires and you would
have to also compare performance of a DS10/DS20 with the bloated IA64 server equivalent.

Thirdly, it took a while before the alpha architecture was mature enough for
the engineers to add all of the goodies that make wildfire and beyond
possible. Do you seriously believe that Intel will succeed in implementing all
of that right from the start into its bloated IA64 ?

It is one thing to say that in the future, the performance gap will narrow.
But it is another to gove up now because you fear that sometime in the future,
Intel might narrow the gap. I guess that is why they never wanted Alpha to
compete againsdt the 8086, knowing it would narrow the gap when it matured
from a 16 bit controller to a 32 bit chip used on system that stole customers
from the more expensive Alpha.

It will also take a long while before HP-UX and NT could run on a
wildfire-like system and take advantage of their possibilities.

And frankly, I just don't see what the big fuss is about Wildfires. If you
split your wildfire into multiple instances of VMS each with its local memory,
doesn't the wildfire simply become an economic decision because it costs less
to run many instances packaged into a single wildfire as opposed to running on
different separate boxes ?

What percentage of wildfire owners actually use all that shared memory stuff
which Andrew so happily pointed out was much slower than anticipated ? And if
the bottleneck in a wildfire isn't the CPU but memory and disk access, then
why not stick an 8086 in there and achieve the same performance ? That seems
to be the argument given by that Fenwick fellow. If an inferior chip will
provide the same performance in a wildfire, perhaps it is the whole wildfire
concept that needs to be re-evaluated.

Another aspect: what if VMS is able to make the fullest use of a Wildfire with
multiple instances each accessing their own local memory and limiting access
to "far away" memory, but HP-UX won't be able to do that. If VMS is expected
to go away, then you are stuck with a big white elephant called a wildfire
that cannot be used to its fullest extent because there is no OS capable of
using it to its fullest extent. At that point, having a super duper chip
becomes moot.


