"Jeff Killeen" <Jeff@IDM-IO.com> wrote in message
news:xtTL7.1615$3n6.134522@typhoon1.gnilink.net...

...

> Since Fenwick's name is now being mentioned in public I have heard from
> enough Compaq sources for it to be super credible that what prompted the
> whole review of the viability of Alpha was Fenwick's work on the follow-on
> to the Wildfire.  Something that keeps getting lost in these internet
> discussions is that systems, and not chips, is what customers buy.  It is
> the end result system, such as Wildfire, that really delivers the
> performance.

Excellence in system design can certainly make an average processor look
good - witness SGI.  But of course it can make an excellent processor look
far better - and can only make a sub-standard processor like Itanic look
marginally acceptable.

  It was Fenwick's team that came to the conclusion that the
> Alpha based follow-on to the Wildfire wouldn't offer a sufficient
> performance advantage over the IA64 servers that Compaq is also
developing.

Wrongo.

There's no mention yet by Intel of any Itanic product that can hold a candle
to EV7 in a large server, because of EV7's on-chip glue to enhance memory
access and multi-processor configurations.  And even Compaq is still
contending that EV7 will be a winner that will easily hold the fort until
the (optimistic) 2004 -5 date (bet on 2005 - and then only because of the
massive infusion of talent Compaq gave up) when Compaq (but not to the best
of my knowledge Intel) has said that the first Itanic with Alpha engineering
influence should wallow into the ocean.

So if any such decision was reached, it *definitely* didn't apply to the
Wildfire follow-on (Marvel, with EV7s for its first engines), or to anything
else before the second half of this decade (the earliest one could have
expected such on-chip glue from Intel *without* the Alpha engineering
infusion).  And since EV8 would have presented pretty much the same face to
the outside world that EV7 did (i.e., its enhancements were primarily
internal - e.g., SMT), there's every reason to believe that its improvements
would have merged transparently right into the same high-performance system
architecture that EV7 pioneered (and that Itanic couldn't have even started
to take advantage of for at least a year or two after EV8 appeared).

>
> I know enough of Fenwick to know...
>
> 1) He had the data and folks outside of Compaq don't

But the Alpha engineering team certainly did, and without exception they
have said that Compaq's assertions were bullshit.

>
> 2) He looked at this from the standpoint of the end product system
delivered
> to the customer and not just esoteric chip issues

While the design of the wagon they pull is also important, the difference
between a Clydesdale and a profusely-sweating pig as motive power does not
exactly qualify as 'esoteric'.

>
> 3) There was no reason to expect he would fall on his sword and kill his
> project i.e. there was nothing anti-Alpha about the guy
>
> ...remember folks that is NOT chip to chip but server to server for the
same
> dollars - PLEASE READ THAT AGAIN - IT IS SERVER TO SERVER FOR THE SAME
> DOLLARS THAT CUSTOMER LOOK AT...

And so far Alpha is 'way out in front in both performance and
cost-performance metrics.  That will continue with EV7 unless Compaq decides
to soak its captive Alpha customers even more than it does already (say
moo).  That would have continued again with EV8, especially given that Intel
would have had to compete against rather than take advantage of the Alpha
team's expertise.

The only area where the cost difference between an Alpha processor and a
smoking brick of death would make any noticeable difference (even if you
ignore Alpha's significant per-processor performance advantage) is in a
low-end 64-bit platform where no special high-performance multi-processor
system glue is required and commodity components can be used.  Of course,
Alpha hasn't made any money in that market segment for a long time if ever,
and Itanic will have to compete there with sub-$1K SPARC boxes, its IA32
brethren, and (one hopes) Hammer (all of which should have noticeably lower
per-processor costs than Itanic and *at least* equivalent performance).

So there is still no reason whatsoever to believe that Alpha would have had
any problem for the foreseesable future in attracting customers away from a
cruise on the Itanic on the basis of both performance and cost-performance.
I suppose there remains a possibility that Fenwick knows something no one
else does - but as long as he keeps it to himself (which makes it rather
difficult to evaluate, let alone question) I suspect that opinions here
won't change much.

- bill

