"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
> To be fair to Intel, those folks have done a pretty decent job cranking the
> clock on their IA32 stuff for the past couple of years... on a MHz basis
> they overtook and blew by Alpha (and everyone else) and haven't looked back.

But how long did it take Intel to take the 8086 game controller and convert it
to something that gained some respect and eventually overtook Alpha ?  They
were able to evolve a very simple architecture.

But IA64 has a lot of heavy baggage with it, and I have to wonder how much
that will hinder progress for that chip.

If Intel is to rely mostly on process to make a chip run at a faster clock
rate to increase performance, it will cost Intel a hell of a lot of money
whenever their FAB can't keep up and a new even more expensive FAB must be built.

Compare this with Alpha which was able to achieve much of its performance
without incredible clock rates. IA64 will turn out quite expensive for Intel
if it must build new FAB plants to keep up with Power.

Another serious consideration: with the home PC market going to be saturated
in a few years, the PC business will consist mostly of replacements. Look at
the current Dell ads on TV with that guy on a sofa telling folks it is time to
replace their old PCs.

In the past, the huge volumes of 8086s made it possible for Intel to pay for
new FABs without much impact on chip prices. But if the volumes of 8086s and
IA64 remains stable as a replacement market, then Intel may not be able to
spend the megabucks required to improve IA64's performance by improving the
fab process.

Meanwhile, IBM and formerly Alpha had designed their chips to be less reliant
on process for performance allowing them to be cost effective under lower volumes.


