Allow me to suggest another line of thinking.  I believe BOTH HP and Compaq
basically have come to the same conclusion from different directions - the
marketplace no longer values leading edge hardware.  That in fact hardware
is rapidly becoming something the market only values at commodity pricing.
This perception is counter to the soul of both Compaq and Digital but
something the market is proving out.  For those of you who aren't long time
Compaq customers Compaq became what it did be being the first in many cases
to bring leading edge technology to PC's.  Compaq's business model was based
on the customer will pay a premium price for systems that push technology
edge.  Dell's overtaking of Compaq's PC business was very much a result of
Compaq's business model becoming invalid in the marketplace.  The PC
marketplace switched from valuing premium technology to valuing price
because the commodity technology was perceived as being good enough.  If you
are Compaq once that happens to you on desktops and low end servers you lay
awake at night wondering when Linux and Windows XX will eat your mid to high
end premium servers.

The 6/25/2001 wasn't about Intel controlling Compaq.  It was about Compaq
coming to believe the marketplace will no longer pay a premium for premium
hardware because the marketplace thinks that commodity hardware is good
enough.  Remember Compaq isn't run by a hardware or software engineer these
days - it is run by a former customer.  Once one sees the decision in the
context that premium hardware is not valued by the marketplace all of
Compaq's decisions make sense.  Now one may disagree with basic premise but
within the context of the premise it makes sense...





