In article <W8UL7.1624$3n6.134716@typhoon1.gnilink.net>, Jeff Killeen wrote:

[snip]

> 
> ....remember folks that is NOT chip to chip but server to server for the same
> dollars - PLEASE READ THAT AGAIN - IT IS SERVER TO SERVER FOR THE SAME
> DOLLARS THAT CUSTOMER LOOK AT...
>
For a brand new system, with all new applications, maybe. And then Sun and IBM 
et cetera are also in the bidding.

What's missing from your equation is the fact that we customers out here have 
many millions invested in applications software. Figures that make the price of 
a top end server look like peanuts.

20 or so years ago it was the norm to move to a different platform every few 
years and rewrite everything in the process, hopefully improving the 
application along the way. Little did we think that the applications we wrote 
then might still be in use today (hence much of the Y2K problem - many assumed 
that a given application would be completely rewritten by that stage).

But we've moved on from that model. If a change of chip means porting, it is no 
longer true that customers only look at "server to server for the same 
dollars".

Want a real life example? We have an application which we considered porting 
from VMS a couple of years ago. The estimated cost came in at several million 
dollars, although the project leader told me it could easily run to much more 
than that due to the application's complexity. So it stayed on VMS.

Force us to port it due to a different chip and yes, we might well spend tens 
of millions doing that (think testing, testing, testing, as well as development 
and more testing). However if we feel _forced_ to port by Compaq/HP decisions, 
you can bet a large chunk of that sum we are going to go Sun / IBM / whatever 
else is available at that point.

So from the perspective as a customer with a heavy investment in applications 
which satisfy our business needs today I am saying that "IT IS MUCH MORE THAN  
SERVER TO SERVER FOR THE SAME DOLLARS THAT CUSTOMERS LOOK AT..."
___
Paul Sture
Neither a "3rd shift operator" nor a "bitter ex-Digital employee" :-)



