> within the context of the premise it makes sense...


Your hypothesis probably holds true in the PC and general low-end of the
business.  Anything that works is good enough.  I think though that you
have identified just one of several factors. 

My list (and probably very incomplete) is ...

- late 90's were a boom time for PC sales because of Y2K issues.  I am
not aware of any PC company who acknowledged the temporary nature nature
of this, either at the time or since.  If they'd been smart they would
have identified this at the time and made only temporary expansions of
their companies.  

- Year 2000 saw the IT industry riding the internet wave and ignoring
the laws of "economic gravity" which say hot air will only support you
so far and then you have to start making real money.  While PCs should
have sold well - they must have because Comaq made a profit on them -
the bigger sellers would have been web servers (which might be why
Compaq's "Enterprise" segment almost doubled their 1999 profit).

- Year 2001 has seen the internet wave (unsurprisingly) come crashing
down taking the IT industry and the general economy with it.  IT gear
was a basic need of every dot com, but with the collapse we see reduced
demand and we see a market flooded with second-hand IT gear.  (I hadn't
thought of it before, but perhaps the $697 million discrepancy between
the income in Compaq's original year 2000 accounts and the income in the
April 2001 revision of their segments could be "bad debts" from failed
dot com companies.  $421 million of the discrepancy was in
"Enterprise".)

- As you say, there is an increasing attitude, especially at the low
end, that anything that does the job is good enough.  (Hell, I'm writing
this on a Windows98 system - it's far from perfect but I am familiar
with the bugs and the solutions.  I see no need to swap to a whole new
set of bugs...)  With the general economy in a slump most companies are
asking the serious questions about the benefits of upgrading their PCS. 
Do most of the staff really need 1.4Ghz systems for Word documents and
email ?  The answer has to be No.

- To some extent Microsoft have lowered expectations about windows-based
operating systems through the highly publicised stability and security
problems.   No wonder "near-enough is good-enough" for commercial and
personal comsumers; that does seem to be Micrsoft's attitude too.

- Also on Microsoft, IIRC, they have adjusted their charging/payments
system in order to preserve their income.  The market is so competitive
that manufacturers have been forced to absorb some of these modified
charges, thus reducing their margins even further.

- PC manufacture and distribution is a labour-intensive business. 
Assembly, transport, warehousing, sales distribution and (of course) all
cost effort, time and money.  The high-end doesn't suffer anywhere near
as much for an initial sales, then unlike PCs there's every chance of
ongoing income from that sale in the form of maintenance, support,
software licensing and if you are lucky, permanent on-site support ....


If this posting makes you queston - yet again - the competence of Compaq
management then I know you are not alone.  Compaq's fixation with PCs
has got them into this mess.  They honestly cannot see any further than
the PC on the desk in front of them.

High-end systems such as VMS are far more stable, have few security
problems, produce better initial profit, produce ongoing income ...  

(I get depressed when I keep stating the bleedin' obvious, trying to get
a blind and deaf management team to take notice...)


John McLean


