"John McLean" <mcleanj@swissonline.delete.ch> wrote in message
news:3C00D95B.6858861E@swissonline.delete.ch...

...

> In short, would the difficulties between the Alpha and the "follow-on"
> to Wildfire be better solved by rethinking parts of the Wildfire concept
> rather than changing processor ?  Especially changing to a processor
> whose capabilities can only be described right now as "wish-ware".

Jeff has no clue about this area.  The 'follow-on' to Wildfire is Marvel,
which is specifically designed around EV7 and is still being touted by
Compaq as *the* place to be until their optimistic estimate of 2004 - 5 when
Itanic supposedly magically grows hydro-foil water-wings and suddenly
catches up.

And there *are* no difficulties taking the next Alpha step (EV8) down that
road, because EV8 was designed to present essentially the same interface to
the surrounding box that EV7 did, so would slip right in and deliver its
improved performance immediately.

Itanic (right through the Madison/Deerfield members, which are McKinley
semi-clones), however, won't slip into a Marvel box - or anything like the
Marvel architecture - at all without added glue to replace what is on the
EV7 chip but is not on the Itanic chip.  There is no chip yet on the Intel
road map that can match EV7 in such areas (let alone EV8's additional
features), and thus - even leaving aside Itanic's major per-processor
performance deficit compared with Alphas that extends as far into the future
as either company had been willing to provide information - the suggestion
that any Itanic would have *ever* fitted into the Marvel architecture
anywhere nearly as well as, let alone better than, Alpha is ludicrous.

Wildfire, in part because of its delay in reaching the market, turned out to
be a bit of a disappointment in areas such as memory access latency
(possibly a bit in bandwidth as well).  The on-chip features in EV7 should
dramatically alter that situation in Marvel.  The absence of those features
on Itanic chips seems likely to make Itanic-crippled Marvel systems perform
much more like Wildfires (but with slower processors than the then-current
Alphas).

And (to address some FUD from another portion of the discussion) the
suggestion that Itanic-based servers will be able to offer better
cost/performance metrics than Alpha-based servers holds no water either.
Not only will it take something like twice as many Itanic processors to
provide equivalent performance (and server prices usually rise more than
linearly with the number of processors, due to the added effort required to
glue them together effectively - *especially* lacking the kinds of on-chip
support that EV7 provides but Itanic does not), but the price difference
between an N-processor Alpha box and an N-processor Itanic box (offering
dramatically less capability) should be minimal for anything but very small
values of N, because processor prices (assuming that Itanic's ever *do* come
down) just don't contribute much to the cost of a mid-range or larger
server.

So to suggest that users would not have been willing to pay a 'premium' for
better technology is a red herring:  Alpha servers can and could in the
future offer significantly better raw cost/performance at the hardware
level - meaning that *Itanic* systems would be those demanding a premium
(and making the additional advantages offered by the better operating
systems on Alpha platforms pure gravy).

In sum, Compaq's arguments in these areas don't stand up to scrutiny any
better this time around than when they were presented last summer.  And they
won't the next time it trots them out either unless and until it adds
significant *new* information content that could alter the underlying
analysis.

- bill





