My opinion (based on discussions with CPQ folks) is that the EV8 folks had
encountered no show-stoppers and may well have been on track. I'll give
Compaq the benefit of the doubt when they claim that EV8 would have
required flawless and timely execution. I'll also accept the claim that
EV8 would have required incremental design resources and money had the
project gone forward.

Regardless of what Compaq says about a "technical decision," I think
economics loomed large in the decision. As a complete architectural
redesign, EV8 would have been a difficult undertaking. And given the
woefully limited market addressability of Alpha (sans Windows support as a
volume expansion solution) I believe Compaq did the math and concluded
that a continued. post-EV7 effort was not economically prudent.

Could I be wrong? Of course. But I haven't seen any concrete evidence that
this is the case. Should such evidence materialize, I'd be the first to
say that I was wrong. Being wrong is one thing, refusing to admit same in
light of factual data is something else entirely!



