"Peter da Silva" <peter@abbnm.com> wrote in message
news:9tr42k$2n4@web.nmti.com...

...

> I think it's obvious that the gap in performance between Alpha and IA64
> is closing... given the current performance if IA64 one would have to be
> even more skeptical than I to assume that Intel won't do better in round
> two. On the other hand, there's room for an awful lot of closing.

Indeed there is.  And while if McKinley appears on schedule it should indeed
improve matters, when EV7 arrives shortly thereafter the gap will open up
again - possibly even wider than it is today, given EV7's advantages in
on-chip glue support for greatly-improved memory-access latency and
bandwidth plus far better multi-processor integration:  while these may not
improve single-processor SPEC results dramatically, they have significant
real-world performance consequences in many typical server situations.

Then IA64 stagnates through Madison/Deerfield, which are just
process-shrinks of McKinley with some added on-chip cache to take advantage
of the newly-available chip area.  While on the Alpha side EV8 was slated to
appear with SMT, which, again, could have been a major win in common server
applications even if it had less effect on single-thread SPEC numbers
(though the enhanced issue-width would have helped some there as well).  Now
that Intel has the Alpha team it has at least a prayer of shipping
significant enhancements to IA64 during the second half of this decade;
without that team, who knows how long improving such a complex (though
unrewarding) architecture would have taken them - while that team was busily
continuing to enhance Alpha?

So I question whether it is indeed obvious that the performance gap would
have closed (save for brief, unsustained periods), given IA64's and Alpha's
road maps as of June 25th:  in fact, I suspect it would have significantly
widened with EV8 (and possibly even with EV7) for any save FP-style
applications (which typical server environments don't seem to entail).  And
there remains the question of how much of a liability IA64's far higher
thirst for per-processor power and chip area would have been, all other
performance issues aside.

Definitely a case of a silk purse and a sow's ear.  Why anyone still has any
difficulty distinguishing between them is a complete mystery to me.

- bill





