In alt.sys.pdp11 Steve Jones <smj@spamfree.crash.com> wrote:
> The board is marked as an TSV05/M7530-PA on the S-box handles. The
> designation M7530-00 is screened on the board with Rev A02 written
> in.

  That shows up as a TS05 controller here, so I believe that's what it
is.

> Were TSV05's really made with uDB50 connectors? Will this thing
> speak to a Cipher F880 (Pertec i/f, I think?)? Will a KLESI?

  There were a wide variety of cables done for these drives, with the
only thing in common being the 2 50-pin connectors of various form
factors. I expect DEC's prior experience with mounting D50 connectors
on S-box handles (LPV11, etc.) convinced them to try something differ-
ent.

  I don't recall what (if anything) DEC did to the F880 besides painting
it cream instead of the standard black.

  There are two issues with the F880 that can bite you - the first is
that speed control is provided by the host (the TU8x switch speeds on
their own, which can bite you in a different way) and if you have an
early F880 with 3200BPI, it may do unnatural acts with the PE burst at
load point which can confuse some controllers (for example, the Dilog
Q-bus version of the M7545 TU80 controller) if you try to use 3200BPI.
But other than that it should work.

