steve.raymond@compaq.com (Steve Raymond) wrote in message news:<7923c18b.0111190830.6e2298fc@posting.google.com>...
> "Graham" <netafex@hotmail.com> wrote in message news:<POfJ7.22839$cG3.3755736@news20.bellglobal.com>...
> > I am attempting to recover an extremely rare music synthesis system which is
> > based on a DEC PDP-1123 running the RSX-11 operating system. I am looking
> > for anyone with indepth knowledge of the hardware and software that maybe
> > able to point me in the right direction with a few initial problems

> > The initial stumbling blocks seem to revolve around communication problems
> > between the PDP and a dumb terminal using a serial port connection. If you
> > can help please let me know and I will forward more specifics of the exact
> > trouble.
> 

Way back in '86 I managed a PDP 11/70 running RSX11M+ with multiple
dumb terminals.  Due to the time lapsed, I'm not really an expert any
more [it's amazing the similarity of current VAX/Alpha VMS to the old
RSX11M+ op sys commands]... but, to connect a dumb terminal to a
serial port you need only 3 wires connected to pins 1, 2, and 3 of an
RS 232? 25 pin connector.  Pin 1, as I recall is the ground or common.
 Pins 2 and 3 are send and receive respectively, (or the other way
around - check a manual).  The trick is that you need to connect pin 2
from the CPU to pin 3 on the terminal and pin 3 to pin 2.  This
reversal is, as I recall, called a "null modem".

If this doesn't work, and it shd, also try connecting pin 7 to 7 and
20 to 20 and 21 to 21 (not sure of the 21 to 21).

For the software, on the CPU, you will need to externally identify
which TT##: you are actually using and do the software terminal setup
to match your dumb terminal's configuration, specifically, baud rate,
parity, stop bits (suggest 9600, none, 2).  I think it was something
like SET TERMINAL but can't remember for sure.  Try something like
SHOW DEVICE (*)/FULL to see a list of devices the system knows about. 
Also, I think the offset is from zero not one, so when you are
counting the physical serial ports start at 0  (ie TT00:), but zero is
usually dedicated to the operating console (system terminal).

Hope this helps.  eaprice@home.com


