The RQDX3 has credits equal to the number of packets it can buffer
internally.  On the very first reply, to the Set Ctrl Char, the RQDX3
awards as many of its credits to the host, within the overall protocol
limit of 15 for the field in the message header, as it can.
Thereafter, it continues to deliver credits to the host until it has
allocated all of them to the host.  After that, it provides one per
returned packet.

In C-like terms, if rqcredit is its initial allocation.

if (resp pkt is end pk && resp pkt is sequential msg) {
	cr_to_send = (rqcredit > 14)? 14: rqcredit;
	rqcredit = rqcredit - cr_to_send;
	insert (cr_to_send + 1) into resp packet;  }

The host receives all the credits up front; thereafter; it receives
one additional credit for each response packet sent over.

The unit poll goes 1, 2, 3... because the GUS is being done with the
"next unit" characteristic.  When VMS sends over unit 4, the RQDX3
wraps the unit number back to 0, changes the unit number in the
response packet, and reports on unit 0.  This is how VMS knows that
the end of the controller has been reached.

Unit #s are keyed to the controller address.  The primary controller
is units 0..3.  At the secondary address, the units are 4..7, etc.

/Bob

