In article <915EA5D50PriitLilleleht@194.126.101.124>,
Priit.Lilleleht@energia.ee (Priit Lilleleht) wrote:

> Hello!
> 
> Just salvaged a DEC 3000/400 from going to trash, the first 64-bit 
> computer in my collection. Can anybody tell me how to access it thru a 
> serial port? All the error lights remain lit after power-on and no data 
> is coming out of the D-25 COMM-port. 
> 
> There are no keyboard and mouse so I've tried: 
> *mouse/keyboard loopback (pins 2-3 and 6-7 connected)
> *removing the video board
> *S3 switch on down position

The console port is the 6-pin connector with the picture of the printer
and two horizonal arrows below it.  It is an "MMJ" (modified modular jack)
connector.  The system will use this console port if it powers up with the
S3 switch in the "down" position.  The console port is meant to connect to
a terminal like a VT420, or any serial port with a terminal emulator.

The 25-pin connector is not the console port.

The keyboard and mouse connect to the 15-pin connector via a special
adapter cable.  These (and the graphics board) are used as the console if
the S3 switch is "up".

> Still, no success, all error lights are lit. In some manuals jumper 
> W2 is referred, but I have not found any 4-pin jumper in this machine. 
> There is still a row of jumpers marked '200 MHz' and 'only one jumper',
> does anybody have information about this jumper? As the machine was 
> taken to pieces this could be set wrong, as it should run a 133 MHz 
> processor. 

There is a jumper somewhere to write-protect the flash ROM, and a jumper
to enable console security.  The 8-position jumper controls what version
of the initial load software gets loaded into the CPU from the serial
ROM.  All the non-default positions are for special diagnostics and
manufacturing tests.

The 8 status LEDs won't go off until the OS turns them off.  I don't
recall the normal pattern of lights when the console is in control.

I found the manual for the DEC 3000-600 and 3000-700 systems here:
    http://www.compaq.com/alphaserver/download/dec3000.pdf
These systems are almost idential to the -400.  They are just faster, and
have a more advanced SCSI controller.

If you want to use the system with OpenVMS or Tru64 Unix, you may need to
update the system firmware first.  You'll be able to see the current
firmware version when you see the console output.

