Again, history is not what matters here:  if the right way to proceed at
this point in time is to recross the river, then the bridges that were
burned will just have to be rebuilt.  And those responsible for burning
them, and lying about it in the process, should be sacked:  it wasn't 'just
a mistake', and if it doesn't cost the corporation its life then it will
come close.

>
> Given the apparent business and economic realities, do you have a viable
> proposal for reinvigorating Alpha? I'm not trolling, I find the concept to
> be interesting, and would find it even more interesting if it could be
> justified.

Resurrect the EV8 development effort, using the EV7 team plus any EV8
engineers who can be attracted back.  Make public any elements of the Intel
deal that would impede this effort (among others, the FTC should have an
interest in them), and if there *are* any significant impediments explain
whether the deal has been so fully consumated that it can't be backed out of
without severe legal exposure.

Given that Alpha sales appeared to be holding their own against the general
industry contraction in Q2 (and certainly by comparison with sales of PCs
and 'industry-standard' servers), the apparent drastic fall-off in Q3 lends
a lot of credence to the contention that a great deal of revenue and profit
was lost due to the decision to cut Alpha development off at the hip.  At
least some of that business should be retrievable if Compaq can demonstrate
clearly that Alpha does have a lengthy future after all:  EV7 appears about
3 years ahead of any performance-comparable IA64 product, and even if EV8
slipped until 2005 it would still be at least a year ahead of any
performance-comparable IA64 product - leaving aside the consequences of
IA64's considerably greater consumption of both power and chip area for a
given performance level.

So Alpha is still a leadership product and should remain so if development
continues.  People attracted to it over the years will continue to be
attracted to it, and with any decent marketing effort its sales should
expand.  Conversely, under current conditions its sales are obviously
contracting:  the difference between the two sales curves should more than
fund its resurrection even now - though obviously it would have been a hell
of a lot better to have skipped the June 25th debacle and garnered the
difference in revenue and profit.

The alternative?  Continue losing Alpha customers - mostly to other
vendors - and their service and PC-related business.  Continue to watch
Compaq ignore VMS rather than leverage those areas in which it retains
leadership and add those features that will allow those strengths to be used
in new markets.  Either see Compaq absorbed into HP and watch the combined
entity flounder for a couple of years of assimilation while the rest of the
industry passes it by, or see the merger die and Compaq left at least
severely injured (or bought at a discount and broken up for parts).

I don't give a damn what happens to the Wintel parts of Compaq except as
that affects the enterprise parts, since only the latter have any unique
value to the industry in my book.  Tandem could probably be broken off and
kept running by any competent party, but Alpha is a complex synergy of
hardware, software, and service that can't survive without a large part of
Compaq surviving (and being able to sell Wintel stuff to enterprise
customers who want that too has some value, as IBM knows).

- bill





