I read the above interchange as suggesting that it's more likely the
mechanics of the proceedings (rather than any 'sensitive' intelligence that
might be involved) that might be damaging, and if so I agree.  Our
government has put itself in a situation where it absolutely *must* control
the trial in a manner reminiscent of the best Soviet 'show trials' of the
bad old days, and this need is at least somewhat inconsistent with a
judicial system that prides itself on its independence from the Executive
branch.

A normal civilian trial, for example, would immediately come up against the
issue of venue:  just as trial locations are often moved to a different city
or even out of state to ensure fairness for a defendant accused of a
particularly heinous crime, in this case it would be eminently reasonable
for the defense to request trial outside the U.S. - and not only is this
diametrically opposed to the intent the Shrub has already expressed, but it
would raise the real possibility that if the all the evidence is as tenuous
as that which has already been made public Bin Laden might actually be
acquitted, either on some technicality or for real.

Consider the implications of that.  It would, for example, retroactively
eliminate at least the stated *legal* justification for our invasion of
another country and deposition of its government, not to mention highlight
the cavalier nature of our presumption of the right to go after terrorism
wherever we happen to think we see it, hard evidence be damned.

A military trial eliminates all that uncertainty and danger (danger at least
to the world reputations of our fearless leaders).  While our government may
state that the reason for it is national security, I place about as much
credence in that as I do in Compaq's assertions that Alpha had to be killed
because it just couldn't keep up with the Itanic juggernaut.

