I should know better than to get back into this but it troubled me
greatly that one of the last guided missiles fired at Kabul as the
Northern Alliance entered scored a direct hit on the Arabic satellite
channel studios totally destroying it. The BBC studio was 50 metres
away and UK residents will likely have seen the clip of the explosion
and blast damage to the BBC studios as the BBC reporter (on air live
with BBC World at the time) was showered in broken glass and debris.
Also seriously damaged was the Associated Press offices. To the best
of my knowledge CNN had not been allowed to stay in Kabul so naturally
they had no offices to damage.

Obviously reporting from a war zone is extremely dangerous but the US
knew exactly where the press were and had managed to avoid hitting
them up to now. It doesn't help world public opinion if you destroy
the main Arabic news channels offices and seriously damage those
belonging to the BBC and AP.  Someone should have been moe careful
with the targeting of the last missiles.

According to the BBC the Taleban had almost completely withdrawn from
Kabul by this point.

Don't know how much of the BBC or similar footage was shown in the
States but it was quite dramatic as they provided live or near live
coverage from Kabul of the Taleban retreating. On the plus side
electricity was restored in Kabul last night so humanitarian issues
have obviously not been ignored.

www.bbc.co.uk/news for BBC coverage.
http://news.bbc.co.uk/olmedia/1650000/video/_1654928_kabul_omah22_vi.ram
for the video of the blast hitting the BBC.

 

--
Alan


