Dennis,

Check the manufacturing date on the back of the monitor.  It is at least 4 years
old, possibly older.  To give you a basis of comparison, I recently sold a pair
of year-old 21" NEC monitors for $300 and $325.  (I'm all out, but I do have a
very nice 18-month-old 20" Sony available for delivery or pick up in the metro
Boston area.)  If the monitor is more than 5 years old, I'd say it is not worth
$200.  I do not have direct experience with that specific model.

Power up the monitor and check it for any signs of burn.  One way to do this is
to operate it with a Wintel machine and set the background color to white.  This
reveals even the smallest flaws, because it pushes all the color guns.  I know
that the VMS and Tru64 folk will cringe at the idea of testing on a Wintel
computer, but, hey, it works.

You don't want something that will become 70 lbs of hazardous waste in a matter
of months... Ben Myers

