I'd forgotten about the drywall/Novell server story.  However, a small
Unix system I built at a former employer whose sole purpose was to sit
on an exterior network segment and relay network outage notices to my
(and then my successor's) pager was in fact bricked up inside a wall (well,
cosmetic brickwork was done over the doorway *we* had used to enter the
network closet it was in, anyway) for a number of years and continued to
function.  It booted from a small SCSI hard disk which it immediately spun
down and ran from RAM; the campus networking folks noticed it was there,
finally, when they had to unseal the only other entrance to the closet in
question after a power outage at least a year after the company I'd worked
for left the building.

It definitely ran unattended for several years, faithfully relaying email.
It was basically bricked up inside a wall (the other door to the space it
was in was basically an access hatch, and was sealed with bolts from the
inside which had to be cut to gain entry) for at least a year.  It ran
just fine.

I am told that, sadly, it did not reboot cleanly when the power was turned
back on -- because its hard drive didn't spin up again after being spun
down for so long.

Interestingly, the machine hardware was notoriously unreliable (a DEC
Multia with the infamous "heat death" syndrome) but we'd had the bad
chips responsible replaced by a tech in the EE department (I worked in
a research park at a large university at the time) when the machine
failed while in an earlier job as my desktop machine, and it was rock
solid from then on.

As far as I know, /tmp never filled up, either (thanks for playing).

