There's a psychological technique to bring people on side. Sometimes
known as the "foot in the face" technique ("foot in the door" being a
more common method). This involves giving someone really bad news
which upsets them then later providing another option which gives some
path forward. The target then will jump enthusiastically on that
option even though they might have rejected it out of hand earlier.

Compaq wanted out of the Alpha business and very senior Alpha
management have known this with near certainty (or possibly even
certainty) for a long time. Faced with the prospect of losing
everything (including VMS) or supporting (and even appearing to
initiate) the new policy most will choose the latter. They may even
convince themselves it was the right path all along.

Sort of like the Stockholm syndrome with hostages.

Had Compaq really wanted to stick with Alpha it would have gone back
to this "senior engineer" and said "Hmm we have problems with Alpha.
How much extra funding do you need to fix this and here it is." By
massaging business unit profitability figures given to employees
Compaq made it appear that the additional money option could not even
be considered. Ergo cancel Alpha.

QED

