Trent,

The facts are 

(a) there is still no evidence bin Laden that would stand up in court;
there are assertions, there are circumstantial and vague connections;
there is NO public evidence.

(b) The statement that you quote is inconclusive regardless of how much
you might wish/hope it was otherwise.  If you were to say, as an
American, "We beat Russia in the basketball." it would be impossible
from this context to determine if you were actually in the team and
played an active part, or if you were speaking generally and really
meaning that America beat Russia.  The big difference is the size of the
collective group encompassed by the word "we".

In bin Laden's case he could easily have been speaking in terms of a
broad group (or groups) of terrorists, none of which directly involves
him in any kind of active role. 


The reaction of the USA is a big concern to many people.  Rather than
become embroiled in recycled arguments of a few months back, let me put
an analogy in computing terms...

If you managed a computer system and one day found that a certain user
could breach the security whenever they wanted to, would you ...
(a)  disable the user's account and perhaps punish them in additional
ways
(b)  discover how they did it and take steps to close the security hole
?

Your answer is ???


With regard to terrorists, the USA appears to be mainly concerned with
(a) and are paying lip-service to (b).  Part (b) is very difficult on a
national basis.  The USA is losing the war against drugs and if this is
an example of the best levels of effort, then it is almost inconceivable
that the country can be truly secured against all forms of terrorism.

In this case (as with drugs) one must work to remove the motivation and
incentive for these illegal activities.  Remove the demand and you don't
just stop the current incident but you lessen the chances of others
undertaking similar activities in future.

This removal of motivation is where many of us feel that the USA is
failing and consequently we fear of other acts of terrorism.


John McLean


