Rob Young wrote:
>         the trial judge let the cat out of the bag on Bin Laden's recorded
>         satellite phone conversations...

I am sorry, but that doesn't impress me. The USA wants to keep secret the
accuracy of its spy satellites. Do I care whether can they see a pimple on my
face or just get enough details to see that I am walking out of my home ? I
know that the USA can monitor anyone they want.

Anyone who has seen James Bond moviesor any other spy movies probably wouldn't
be so impressed with the actual US spy capabilities. Heck, if aliens came to
earth, we'd be saying things like "what, no replicators ? No hollow-deck ?,
geez, you guys aren't very advanced for a space faring civilisation!" ....

It wasn't so much the revelation that the USA was capable of listening in on
phone conversations (geez, big news here!) but the fact that they had named
Bin Laden and were targetting him. That is why he started to live a very
covert life since he new know that the USA was looking for him.

The military has very old thinking when it comes to technology. Remember that
they used to be the sole users of computers and were far ahead of everyone
else. But nowadays, the military is generally behind private corporations in
terms of technology. Look at how difficult it was to get the military to agree
to remove those silly encryption export restrictions. They still thought that
their own encryption algorythms were so fancy that nobody else in the world
could possibly develop something as or stronger. Meanwhile, stronger
encryption was widely available on the 'net which doesn't have boundaries.

Same with GPS. The military insisted on keeping SA (reduction of accuracy on
purpose) on, this way, the military would have an edge on anyone else because
they had the codes to use the accurate service. But civilians developped DGPS
services that went around the silly SA scrambling. It took a few years for the
military to agree that SA had become moot and that it should be turned off
(Thanks to Bill Clinton for that BTW).

We already have seen commercially avalable satelite photographs that give good
enough resultion. Does it really matter if the USA can see through your
clothes and get a close up on the tatoo on your shoulder to identify you ?

The only thing that they should keep quiet is the number of satellites they
have and their orbits. This way, I can't use my PDA to calculate when there
would likely be no satellites overhead, allowing me to run to the convenience
store to buy a chocolate bar and then run back home without the USA being able
to take a picture of what type of chocolate bar I just bought.

I just don't see why the military thinks that its technology is so advanced
that it must be kept ultra secret. Once you know that they can take pictures
of you and listen in to any phone conversation, why all the coy measures ?


