Rob Young writes:
> Or so you think.  We know a bit differently.  Depends on what
> technology you are describing.  How about stealth technology that
> the CIA allowed Lockheed Martin to incorporate into the F117A from
> the SR71?
> Does that count as a good counter-example?

Huh?  Lockheed's Skunkworks designed and built both the SR71 and F117A,
using technologies that they invented.  CIA was merely the sponsor & user of
the SR71.  CIA had no reason or power to keep USAF from buying the
Skunkwork's newest concept 17 years later.   So that's not a good example.
I recommend the book "Skunk Works", ghost written for retired manager
Ben Rich by Leo Janos.

The key breakthrough in designing the F117A was invented in Russia,
where a radar mathematician worked out a way to compute actual radar
reflections from any angle (couldn't be computed before).  That first
method only understood faceted shapes, so F117A was designed
without any curves anywhere despite its inefficiencies.
Later reflection algorithms could handle curves, so the B-2 and later
designs went back to using aerodynamic curves.





