> "Terry C. Shannon" <terryshannon@mediaone.net> writes:
>
> > It is intuitively obvious that IBM is a big beneficiary of Compaq's recent
> > strategic decisions.
>
> But, alas, they don't sell Alphas or VMS in most places.
>
> > As a longtime Alpha advocate I too was dismayed about the decision
> > to scuttle the architecture. Especially in light of the fact that I
> > was doing Alpha futures pitches to CPQ audiences as recently as one
> > month pre-Trees (I wasn't alone, there's plenty of Alpha marketeers
> > sporting albumin on their countenances these days, too).
>
> But we will have to deal with the liars who did this for the indefinate
> future.
>
> > Rightly or wrongly, Alpha's dead. Get over it. No amount of 20-20
> > hindsight will change this fact.
>
> > I continue to maintain that a decade of marketing malfeasance and
> > stupid strategy tricks ensured Alpha's ultimate oblivion. And while
> > I'm not a math major, I can do the math when it comes to
> > apportioning an annual ~$250M R&D and infrastructure cost across an
> > annual run rate of perhaps half a million processors.
>
> If you believe their numbers at all.

It is difficult to break out exact numbers from CPQ financial statements,
but an approximation is possible. The $250M figure is not at all
unreasonable. Based on recent IDC data, the Alpha run rate appears to be
significantly lower than the 100K systems per year I postulated. If IDC is
right (and they have folks who count boxes for a living) and if the $250M
figure is anywhere near accurate, the overhead associated with each Alpha
chip Compaq shipped is higher than the $500 figure I used.

>BTW, does anyone know the name of
