On Fri, 16 Nov 2001 03:57:43 GMT, Chris Bailey
<cbailey@nospam.cander.net> wrote:

>Hi,
>	I've got a really stupid question here.  All of my prior
>experience has been with the older MicroVax series, so I'm pretty
>clueless here.  I have a Vaxserver 3100 that's a real server which
>never came with a video connection at all.  I've got 2 connectors on
>the back of this box that I'm still trying to figure out what they
>are.  
>
>	One is a Centronics connector that's exactly like a parallel
>printer connector, but I doubt that's what it is as it doesn't have
>that type of label on it.  Instead it's labeled 4 - 11.  This leads me
>to believe that this might be serial ports 4 - 11.  If so, does anyone
>out there have a pinout for it?  I can make my own cables, but need at
>least a starting point so I don't fry something. 
>
>	The second connector is a 3 row DB type connector with 50
>pins.  I would assume that this would be some kind of expansion or I/O
>connector like would go to a tape or disk drive of some type.  Is this
>worth messing with?  I have plenty of drive strorage and even a tape
>drive and cdrom attached, so don't need it unless it's something
>really cool that I can find some use for.  

Sounds like you've got a DSW or DSH (I forget which) board installed -
the one with 8 async ports and 1 sync port.  If you pop the top off, you
may see that both connectors are ribboned off the same board.

The async ports will be TXA0-7, but you need the fancy "harmonica"
connector/cable which fans out to 8 MMJ ports from the Centronics port.
The sync port is the 3-row connector - usually this would be used for
some WAN link, PSI, X25 etc.


	John


