I agree with Bill in the substance of his arguments, but not necessarily
in the way he chose to express them.

In your SKC Special there are a number of contentious points....

1.  You said "For nearly a decade, Alpha has reigned as the fastest
processor on the planet. Compaq=97and Digital before it=97invested heavil=
y
in a series of misguided marketing campaigns designed to convey this
fact to the marketplace."  Sorry, I must have missed this marketting. =

Where was it ?  On bus-stops in Poland again ??

2. You said "These investments [in Alpha] yielded abysmally low ROI". =

Methinks comparatively speaking, the ROI on Alpha was very good.  From
the Compaq financials, Windows-based products return about 2% on
investment in a GOOD year but Alpha-based systems return about 12 to
15%. =


3.  You said "About two years ago, several senior technologists and
strategists in Compaq=92s High Performance Systems Divisions decided to
take an objective and soul-searching look at the 'Whither=97or
Wither=97Alpha?' question."   I find this very odd because the well-known=

report which compared Alpha to IA64 and concluded that Alpha was far
superior actually has a date stamp of 11 Oct 1999.  I find it very odd
that a report extolling the virtues of Alpha should be released at that
time and at the same time there are questions arising about the future
of Alpha.  (I believe the report escaped into the public arena many
months, by which time - if your statement is correct - Compaq had
already concluded that the future for Alpha was "limited".)

4.  You said "With an installed base of perhaps ~700K systems and an
annual run rate of perhaps ~100K systems, the Alpha architecture
appeared incapable of gaining market share or momentum."  To which I
would add "because of poor or non-existent marketing".  Had Compaq
actually tried to increase the sales through good marketing then your
statement would hold water.  As events stand, this incapability is only
an assertion.

5.  You said "(Further clouding the EV8 issue was anecdotal evidence
that the incorporation of symmetric multithreading technology would have
reduced the processor=92s target frequency by a full gigahertz.)".  This
would be a curious statement if it were true, and would indeed justify
the abandonment of Alpha (assuming of course that SMT was of
sufficiently high priority  ... which must be considered question in
light of the performance penalty it causes.)  On the other hand (IIRC),
we also have anecdotal evidence that EV8 was meeting performance
specifications and schedules...

6.  You said "...the Compaq research team took a close look at the
shifting epicenter of system  differentiation. They discovered that raw
processor performance was ceasing to be a key contributor to enterprise
system differentiation. (If CPU performance really mattered, Compaq
would have eclipsed Sun in the enterprise server space ages ago!)". =

Well maybe Compaq would have eclipsed Sun if they had bothered to
advertise Alpha and Tru64.  (Note we stack one unix gainst another, not
VMS which would force a bigger change on the customer.)  You know, maybe
performance would have been important if Alpha had been promoted hard
enough.  Imagine a system with PERFORMANCE + RAS + NUMA + latency +
bandwidth ...  This conclusion again reflects on the lack of marketing
effort by Compaq.  =


7.  Finally, you assemble a collection of "achievements" of Intel and
conclude "the rationale for the IPF decision is clear."  Sorry, no.  If
achievements in processor technology were all that mattered, then Alpha
would be the preferred option. =



So much of this argument is simply a reflection of Compaq's inability to
promote Alpha and their consequent conclusion that there was no market
for it.  Honestly, it's like me saying that I could never win any awards
for basketball.  That's an obvious conclusion since I don't play
basketball.


John McLean


