Terry C. Shannon <terryshannon@mediaone.net> wrote in message
news:JOSH7.17754$I6.4371753@typhoon.ne.mediaone.net...

...

> Rightly or wrongly, Alpha's dead. Get over it. No amount of 20-20
hindsight
> will change this fact.

That's patently false:  if the decision is perceived as sufficiently bad,
and the economic consequences of it sufficiently drastic to cause a change
of management at Compaq, then change is eminently possible.  Resurrecting
the architecture's viability will certainly be more difficult than it would
have been before last summer's incompetence, but if the alternative is even
bleaker then it can happen.

>
> I continue to maintain that a decade of marketing malfeasance and stupid
> strategy tricks ensured Alpha's ultimate oblivion. And while I'm not a
math
> major, I can do the math when it comes to apportioning an annual ~$250M
R&D
> and infrastructure cost across an annual run rate of perhaps half a
million
> processors.

Yes, Alpha might have taken over the world with even marginally-adequate
management.  But despite the lack of such, it still turned a tidy profit,
especially in comparison to Compaq's other business, retained the respect of
the industry, and retained the potential to expand its market significantly
at any time should its owner develop minimal competence.

If a processor costs an extra $500, it will admittedly never conquer the
desktop or small-appliance market.  So what?  Alpha's profits never came
from those markets.

Conversely, even if a processor is given away free of charge, it will never
conquer the mid-range-and-up server/high-end workstation market if its
performance sucks - because it will drag down the effectiveness of all the
associated hardware, solftware, and user effort that *isn't* free.

Alpha remained eminently viable, right up until June 25th.  It would be
harder now, but it *still* could be made viable.  And the efforts to make it
so would be likely repaid by nothing more than the reduction in loss of
revenue from VMS and Tru64 sales caused by the June 25th announcement (which
is perhaps the most telling indication of the utter incompetence of that
decision).

- bill





