Could Alpha have done better with better marketing?  Almost certainly.
Would it have done enough better to reverse the decision to kill it?
Likely but not certainly.
Was Compaq capable of doing a better job of marketing it?  Not based
on the evidence.  There was seldom any evidence of marketing at all.

Marketing is a lot more than ads during the Super Bowl.  As the years
went by, information about Alpha's, VMS, storage products, software,
etc., etc. became increasingly hard to obtain.  A major function of
marketing is getting information about your products to your customers
and potential customers.  This has become a black hole.  DECDirect as a
planning tool began to go downhill in the early 90's.  It became
increasingly glossy with ever less information.  It became impossible
to discover costs, performance figures, prerequisits, etc.  I found
the DECDirect CD's to be extrordinarily slow and hard to use, and they
never seemed to have any prices.  The current web site is also very
cumbersome and incomplete compared to the old (text) E-store.  It
also completely lacks prices, which are vital to systems planning.
(If you can't determine the relative cost of a single large system
vs. a 3-node cluster, how do you propose either to a customer?)

The old Systems and Options catalogs where great for technical info,
site planning, etc., (no prices), but acquiring them was always like
pulling hens teeth (why didn't they just mail me a new one every
6 months?)  I haven't seen a new one in about 7 years (1994?)

The other part of marketing, determining customer's needs and
responding to them, has also been lacking.  The recent response
seems to be "if we don't try, we can't fail."  At the same time,
DEC/Compaq has devoted enourmous resources to questionable products.
For example DECnet-5 (OSI), while ignoring TCP/IP.  The only really
useful feature of DECnet-5, from my standpoint, is the ability to
run on a TCP/IP transport, which I think could have been achieved
much sooner if that was the original goal.

I'm not saying they don't eventually reach the right goals, but
they often follow a very slow and circuitous route to get there.
For example, a supported, current Apache server, Java, IP stack,
etc. are worthy items, but slow in arriving.  (Remember how much
mileage or British friend got out of VMS's lagging Java support
about 18 months ago?)  I know some of this is due to the careful
approach of VMS engineering, but I think a lot of it is due to
misplaced priorities, misplaced due to lack of understanding of
their current and potential markets.

> 5.  You said "(Further clouding the EV8 issue was anecdotal evidence
