Intel has produced some lemons, too.  But Terry said "process technology",
not "processor technology".  Process technology is the ability to produce
large numbers of shrinking chips reliably and cheaply (i.e. with good
yields.)  Terry says that Compaq is betting on Intels ability to produce
future processors that achieve better performance by process improvements,
and not necessarily by design improvements.  (Putting the glue logic on the
chip or adding SMT is a design improvements.  Shrinking from .18 to .13
microns, or converting to a new technology like copper is a process
improvement.  Obviously they go hand-in-hand, but Terry is claiming that
Compaq feels that process improvement is one of Intel's strengths.  The
other is deep pockets.)

Speaking of Intel's deep pockets, people were wondering about the lack
of any visible income from Intel in the recent Compaq financial statements.
I wonder if they are funding the VMS and Tru64 ports, and IPF box design
and integration with existing Compaq peripherals?  If so, the income
may appear slowly over time, rather than in a big payment.  Also, if they
