Back around 1988 the Dundee College of Technology 2060 finally was
taken off maint. as it got surrounded by more and more VAXes. Not too
long after it died completely and was officially dead for several
months. I was working elsewhere but was extremely sad to see a machine
I'd used for over ten years die. Three months later I got a call to
come in and as soon as I got out of the lift I thought I could hear
the 20's fans running through two walls and above the level of the
main air-conditioners and all the VAXes.. Put it down to just wanting
to hear it. Then turned a corner, glanced through the machine room
window to see the single System On light glowing red. The FS engineer
(called in for a VAX problem) could not let one of the last remaining
KLs in Scotland sit there dead and had re-jigged power and fans to
bypass failed regulators (without using DEC spares which would have
been theft) and the system ran for almost another two years before
failing beyond volunteer repair.

So yes it was big and noisy but it really could run for a very long
time unattended if some of the more sensitive components were
replaced/bypassed., Who remembers AIRFLOW CPU halts?

Think we even had an UP2LONG BUGHLT as TOPS-20 assumed that if data
structures said it had been up for over a year something was wrong as
PM should have been performed regularly!

