> that a report extolling the virtues of Alpha should be released at that
> time and at the same time there are questions arising about the future
> of Alpha.  (I believe the report escaped into the public arena many
> months, by which time - if your statement is correct - Compaq had
> already concluded that the future for Alpha was "limited".)

You want consistency?  Since June 25th came as a surprise to
practically everyone (including, according to their posts, just
about everyone in the VMS group), Compaq's decision about the
future of Alpha could very well have been completely unknown to
the authors of the Oct 11 report.  (Maybe someone could start a
whole thread about how Compaq operates on the same management
principles as the Mafia?)

***
I suspect John's point is that the radical inconsistency between the report
and the other 'investigation' was at least visible to top management:  their
June 25th decision makes it clear they were aware of the latter, and their
sales tactics make it clear they were aware of the former.  It bolsters the
suggestion that they reached the conclusion they wanted rather than arrived
at it based on the evidence.
***

> 4.  You said "With an installed base of perhaps ~700K systems and an
> annual run rate of perhaps ~100K systems, the Alpha architecture
> appeared incapable of gaining market share or momentum."  To which I
> would add "because of poor or non-existent marketing".  Had Compaq
> actually tried to increase the sales through good marketing then your
> statement would hold water.  As events stand, this incapability is only
> an assertion.

Could Alpha have done better with better marketing?  Almost certainly.
Would it have done enough better to reverse the decision to kill it?
Likely but not certainly.
Was Compaq capable of doing a better job of marketing it?  Not based
on the evidence.  There was seldom any evidence of marketing at all.

Marketing is a lot more than ads during the Super Bowl.  As the years
went by, information about Alpha's, VMS, storage products, software,
etc., etc. became increasingly hard to obtain.  A major function of
marketing is getting information about your products to your customers
and potential customers.  This has become a black hole.  DECDirect as a
planning tool began to go downhill in the early 90's.  It became
increasingly glossy with ever less information.  It became impossible
to discover costs, performance figures, prerequisits, etc.  I found
the DECDirect CD's to be extrordinarily slow and hard to use, and they
never seemed to have any prices.  The current web site is also very
cumbersome and incomplete compared to the old (text) E-store.  It
also completely lacks prices, which are vital to systems planning.
(If you can't determine the relative cost of a single large system
vs. a 3-node cluster, how do you propose either to a customer?)

The old Systems and Options catalogs where great for technical info,
site planning, etc., (no prices), but acquiring them was always like
pulling hens teeth (why didn't they just mail me a new one every
6 months?)  I haven't seen a new one in about 7 years (1994?)

The other part of marketing, determining customer's needs and
responding to them, has also been lacking.  The recent response
seems to be "if we don't try, we can't fail."  At the same time,
DEC/Compaq has devoted enourmous resources to questionable products.
For example DECnet-5 (OSI), while ignoring TCP/IP.  The only really
useful feature of DECnet-5, from my standpoint, is the ability to
run on a TCP/IP transport, which I think could have been achieved
much sooner if that was the original goal.

I'm not saying they don't eventually reach the right goals, but
they often follow a very slow and circuitous route to get there.
For example, a supported, current Apache server, Java, IP stack,
etc. are worthy items, but slow in arriving.  (Remember how much
mileage or British friend got out of VMS's lagging Java support
about 18 months ago?)  I know some of this is due to the careful
approach of VMS engineering, but I think a lot of it is due to
misplaced priorities, misplaced due to lack of understanding of
their current and potential markets.

***
Very similar to the ground that our group covered in its presentation to
Capellas 18 months ago.  Zero interest in response.  When your customers are
pounding on your door trying to tell you how to improve sales and you thumb
your nose at them, it's a fairly direct indication of your future plans for
the products in question and lack of interest in exploring expansion.

In other words, while it's arguable that DEC was partly flat-out incompetent
in marketing, it's pretty clear that, at least under Capellas, Compaq just
wasn't interested.  Compaq knows how to market other products effectively
and was given plenty of specific input about how to market VMS and Alpha
more effectively.
***

...

Speaking of Intel's deep pockets, people were wondering about the lack
of any visible income from Intel in the recent Compaq financial statements.
I wonder if they are funding the VMS and Tru64 ports, and IPF box design
and integration with existing Compaq peripherals?  If so, the income
may appear slowly over time, rather than in a big payment.  Also, if they
are funding Tru64, what effect would that have on the speculated demise
of Tru64 following the HP merger?

***
Suggesting that this is reasonable compensation for killing Alpha is
circular:  if Alpha hadn't been axed, the ports would have been at best in
the 'might be nice' category rather than in any way of primary importance,
as would the IPF box design.
***

> So much of this argument is simply a reflection of Compaq's inability to
> promote Alpha and their consequent conclusion that there was no market
> for it.  Honestly, it's like me saying that I could never win any awards
> for basketball.  That's an obvious conclusion since I don't play
> basketball.

But would you win awards if you did play?  ;-)

***
We may not know anything about John's athletic ability, but we *do* know
something about Compaq's marketing ability.  Compaq has in the past
demonstrated the skills it would have taken to market Alpha successfully and
has had plenty of encouragement to put them to use, so his observation seems
appropriate even if his simile had a gap in it.

- bill





