Executive Summary:  I am in Bill�s court here.  Attacking him is
completely out of place and speaks more about the attackers rather
than the target.

In no particular order some comments - some opinions, some abstract
induction.

a) There is much here to support the view that TS is an apologist to
the Compaq     corporate spin as well as that of Intel (hardly
surprising given how TS makes his living - not a criticism, just an
sad observation).  The notion that everything Compaq has said about
the Alphacide is true, which seems to be the underlying premise of the
paper, seems at best only marginally related to reality and would be a
first in the history of spin and certainly an about face in Compaq�s
standards of corporate truth.  I think there is just too much
conflicting evidence/information to swallow Compaqs line, as TS has
done.

b) It seems intuitively obvious (to me at least) that the Compaq spin
is to cover the obvious.  To wit,  IPF = Intel+HP.  A deal with HP
would be politically impossible within HP with Alpha alive.  The
HP-Intel relationship would be also untenable without an Alpha
obituary (and death is required for an obituary). TS�s
rationalisations as to why death was preannounced are irrelevant
sophistry. This spin is post rationalisation at its worst.

c) As an aside, I am still waiting for an engineering debunking of the
the Digital(or was it Compaq) whitepaper analysis of Alpha v
IPF.  This would (were it actually possible) at least add some
