On June 25th Compaq announced the surrender of its Alpha technology to
the technologically inferior Intel. On September 4th it announced the
plan to merge with HP.  A number of people continue to want to believe
that the two were not related and that Carly just happened to call
Curly soon after the sell-out to Intel. Well according to
http://www.zdnet.com/zdnn/stories/news/0,4586,5099466,00.html
the HP board first discussed the proposed merger on July 19th just
three weeks after the Alphacide. As Carly and Curly have helpfully
told us that they discussed the issue privately between themselves for
a considerable time before approaching their boards it would seem
impossible that this discussion did not begin long before June 25th.

No wonder Carly and Curly claim they can't exactly remember when it
all began. Anti-competitive behaviour pure and simple. Black and
White. Let the current team of Carly and Curly away with this and
they'll screw us all again first chance they get. Terry Shannon it's
about time you switched sides and we looked at getting some serious
legal advice. I'd like to see them rot in jail. Not because I'm
vindictive but because they appear to have colluded with Gates and
Intel in a manner highly damaging to their customers and to their own
share price. They have not acted in the best interests of the
shareholders or consumers and have intentionally misled both. It  is
time they were stopped.

Could Compaq have announced the Alphacide in favour of an architecture
co-designed by their prospective partner HP after the proposal became
public and before it was voted through? Judging by the SEC rules it
would seem not. So was it really a conspiracy (oh no he said
conspiracy) to break the rules by other means? Looks like it to me.

You would almost think Wintel really did bribe Curly and Carly
personally. Of course that's unthinkable so we shouldn't even bother
investigating should we?

And I strongly advise any Compaq or HP lawyer reading this not to fire
me off an anonymous piece of seemingly friendly legal advice. Terry's
statement that usenet postings don't get read is just another piece of
his bullshit whether he knows it or not. Any other genuine, interested
legal advice is welcomed.

Maybe we should first contact the individual US States and European
Regulators still pursuing Microsoft as they likely have the guts, the
technical knowledge and the right legal people to really look into
this. 

"There's no going back" said Carly, "We've burned our boats". Actually
you've torpedoed the customers' cruisers (Alpha, OpenVMS, Tru64) but
haven't managed to inflate the replacement rubber dinghy despite the
enormous quantities of hot air blown in its direction. And you expect
us to believe you're happy to have VMS as a passenger in this boat?
Possibly but only to give you one last thing to chuck out later.

To quote the Hitchhikers Guide: Carly and Curly I'd put your personal
analyst on danger money. And now I understand  why Carly keeps
referring to all the analysts she's been talking to. Makes sense that
way...

If legal opinion is that this is interesting but still a bit weak
there's a lot of other evidence regarding the intentional destruction
and non promotion of highly profitable non Wintel Compaq product lines
in order to (one presumes) placate Mr Gates which numerous individuals
could help provide.

DEC (remember them) products were not safe in Compaq's hands. Let's
not let Carly finish the job and HP with it.
--
Alan


