Excellent rejoinder.  But kidding aside, what Barb likely means is that she
*thinks* it was a delaying tactic by the Taliban, since anyone sufficiently
acquainted with their inner councils to know it for a fact would also know
where Bin Laden is and could quickly put an end to at least the current
stage of this affair.  And, as with other aspects of negotiation, Dubya's
approach has made certain that we'll likely never know for sure either way.

Of course, allowing the Taliban to succeed in any such delay could have been
disasterous:  who knows how long Bin Laden might have remained on the loose
with as much as a week or so of extra time?  Oh, wait:  he's still on the
loose anyway, and there was adequate time for discussion during the period
that our forces were being moved into position without losing an extra
minute of time...

A lot of actions, both in the corporate and in the international arena, seem
to make sense as long as you don't examine them very closely.  And such
examination is so tedious for people who have no personal interest in the
issues - it's far easier just to let leaders think for you and adopt their
opinions.

