Compaq Hopes Dim Minus Hewlett-Packard, Investors Say (Update4)
By Peter J. Brennan

Houston, Nov. 8 (Bloomberg) -- Compaq Computer Corp. will have a tough time surviving alone
if its planned $19.9 billion sale to larger rival Hewlett-Packard Co. falls through, and the
prospects for finding another buyer look dim, investors said.

``They'd need a real heavyweight to step in'' if another company is to buy Compaq, said Todd
Ahlsten, research director at Parnassus Investments, which owns 700,000 shares. ``I don't
know who else would have the critical mass to consummate the deal.''

International Business Machines Corp., the biggest computer maker, is big enough, yet
wouldn't want to rely more on low-profit personal computers, investors said. Dell Computer
Corp., the biggest PC maker, prefers building sales to buying rivals, Chief Executive Officer
Michael Dell has said.

Some shareholders have criticized the Compaq purchase since Hewlett-Packard Chief Executive
Officer Carly Fiorina announced it Sept. 3. With relatives of Hewlett-Packard's two founders
opposing the deal, investors say the acquisition is more likely to collapse -- leaving Compaq
without a buyer in the toughest personal- computer market in more than 15 years.

Compaq CEO Michael Capellas decided to sell the company after trying to restructure it three
times in two years. He lost the crown as top PC seller to Austin, Texas-based Dell and has
said sales slipped in recent weeks as customers fretted over which products would emerge at
the combined company after the acquisition.

`No Plan B'

Compaq shares remained unchanged today at $7.99. They had dropped 35 percent since the
company's sale was announced. The shares are trading 31 percent below the value of
Hewlett-Packard's offer, indicating that investors think the deal will unravel.

``There's no Plan B,'' Compaq spokesman Arch Currid said. ``We're fully committed to this
merger.'' He noted that a large investor, billionaire Prince Alwaleed Bin Talal, who says he
owns 1 percent of Compaq, supports the acquisition.

Signing more than $2 billion in contracts since the Hewlett- Packard accord shows Compaq
still draws customers, Currid said.

Hewlett-Packard director Walter Hewlett, his sisters Eleanor Hewlett Gimon and Mary Hewlett
Jaffe, and the William R. Hewlett Revocable Trust said Tuesday that they intend to vote their
5 percent stake against the acquisition. They and the William and Flora Hewlett Foundation
own more than 100 million Hewlett-Packard shares.

David W. Packard's Packard Humanities Institute, which owns 1.3 percent of the shares, also
will oppose the transaction.

``The fact that they agreed to a modest premium shows the internals are pretty tough,'' said
Sunil Reddy, whose Fifth Third Bancorp owns 39,000 Compaq shares.

The David and Lucile Packard Foundation, the company's largest shareholder with a 10 percent
stake, hasn't decided about the purchase. Susan Packard Orr controls the foundation.

Company Directors

Compaq's board said yesterday it supports the acquisition unanimously. Hewlett-Packard's
directors, except Walter Hewlett, also back it.

Hewlett-Packard shares fell 83 cents, or 4.3 percent, to $18.35. They have declined 21
percent since Sept. 3. The acquisition was valued at $25 billion when it was announced.

Capellas and Fiorina say the planned combination's benefits include new services
capabilities, broader product offerings for server computers and a company able to mount a
bigger challenge to Armonk, New York-based IBM.

IBM is the largest seller of computers and related services, though it controls only about
6.6 percent of the PC market, researcher Gartner Dataquest said. A combined Hewlett-Packard
and Compaq would have about 17 percent, though profit margins are shrinking as prices fall
and competition intensifies.

No Other Buyers?

Heavyweights such as IBM have the potential to snap up Compaq if the present deal falls
apart, but they wouldn't, investors said.

``There's no way that IBM would want to increase its exposure to PCs, especially at the
retail end,'' Reddy said. ``Why would you want to dilute your core franchise by going into
the commodity business?''

Dell also is unlikely to bite.

