CEO Dell said in a March speech. ``The best approach for us is to acquire our competitors'
customers one at a time.''

Compaq had a third-quarter loss on a 33 percent sales decline, and analysts expect losses to
continue in the next two periods. Still, Houston-based Compaq doesn't need to sell at any
price, some shareholders said.

Balance Sheet Strength

The company has $3.94 billion in cash and equivalents, a server business that analysts said
is worth more than $8 a share on its own and a brand name that's well regarded by consumers.
Hewlett-Packard backing off wouldn't end that.

``It would be a traumatic experience, but it's not like the company is near death,'' Ahlsten
said. ``They still have a decent balance sheet and quite a bit of cash. I don't see a lot of
risk at buying stock at $8.''

Without Hewlett-Packard, Compaq will require another restructuring, investors said. This
time, the focus needs to be on cutting costs and increasing its direct sales from the current
35 percent of Compaq's total, Lehman Brothers analyst Dan Niles said.

``It will have to adopt the Dell model'' of direct sales, said Niles, who doesn't own Compaq
shares. ``You'll see some major changes to compete with Dell.''

While investors say Fiorina may lose her job if the purchase dies, they said 47-year-old
Capellas has the skill to help Compaq survive alone. Capellas, who became CEO in July 1999,
has built a reputation as a savvy operations manager.

``He's tried to turn around a huge battleship,'' Ahlsten said. ``I hope he survives because
he knows better than anyone what needs to be done.''


