Lets bring in some marketing theory.

The buyers for a product can be divided up into sets
of similar groups.  The needs of each group tends to
be different.  They also buy from different shops
and salesmen.

Common segments are men, women, children, families,
companies, military, civil service and builders.  
There are others.

Companies normally sell a limited range of products in 
one or two segments.  For instance, IBM is one of the
worlds most successful computer makers - it made the
PC respectable.  IBM failed to sell the PC to families,
repeating its failure with typewriters.  That 'B' does
stand for business.

My view of HP is a seller of expensive oscilloscopes
and other tools for engineers.  I see Compaq as a 
seller of cheap PC to the general public via high
street shops.

There is no obvious long term overlap between the two.

VMS is an operating system for companies able to run
batch jobs like the monthly payroll.  It also has
a built in VDU device handler that permits several
people to run programs online.  It may or may not
have heard of a mouse.

The PC is a single user computer that is good at 
playing Tomb Raider.  Unlike some games machines the 
keyboard is also useful for typing letters and
documents.

Windows is a single user operating system that allows
50 year old secretaries to produce the minutes of a
meeting on the same day it appears on her desk.  Use
of a mouse permits the woman to prepare and print
the minutes without having to learn a command language.
The poor batch system means that all actions have to 
be driven by the user (DOS is a little different).

Mail servers can run for 6 months without anyone
needing to give an order; then only to confirm that
the time does need adjusting by 1 hour.

I am a computer programmer who is typing this on a
HP Vectra at work and have a Compaq at home.

Questions

What is HP doing mass producing computers?  They
need a minimum price of say 5,000 dollars just to
pay the salesman commission.

What was Compaq doing buying Digital?  VAXs and
PDP11s are not suitable for housewife's.  The
production lines are set up to make high reliability
machines, not lots of cheap ones.

The future.

I suppose that HP could use VMS to run the main
computer in a laboratory full of automated
test/production  machines.  The network would 
permit the accountants to control the stock from their
desks.

The PC divisions of Compaq and HP could become the
marketing and distribution department of an Asian (low 
cost) manufacturer.  This service company may be an
independent company.

In summary merging HP, Compaq and Digital does not
make sense to me.  By splitting the mess up it may
be possible to produce some sane companies but I
am suspicious.
