Well, I bet that thinking took a real effort...

Look at the figures (annual financial statements) ... 

In the three years (1998-2000), PC's produced revenue of $55.679 billion
but have cost $55.685 billion, thus making a loss of $6 million.  In
that time PCs were the cause of 52% of the total costs but returned 0%
of the total income; Enterprise systems incurred 32.4% of costs but
produced 56% of income; Global services (the other big part) incurred
15.3% of costs and produced 44% of income.

To put it another way, without PCs to influence revenues and costs, over
the 3 years Compaq would have made a profit of $6 billion on expenses of
only $51 billion, not the $106 billion of expenses but no additional
profit when PC's are included in the equation.

Compaq has to face facts.  PC's have big expenses and produce little
return.  It was not so bad when sales volumes were high and the margin
was reasonable, but now the volume is down and the margins are razor
thin.  In the good times (late 1990's) Compaq did fine with PC's but
they are still carrying a lot of the baggage that they accumulated
during those good years.

The problem with PC's is all the costs...
- transport (from the manufacturing Asia, IIRC)
- warehousing (costs of the building and the staff)
- distribution (the contents of each cardboard box is sold separately so
there's a lot of handling)
- loading OS's (per box) 
- sales staff (whose knowledge is limited to PC's)
- marketing (to sell these products but which incurs costs)
- commission to Microsoft
- administration (to look after all of the above)
- out-of-date inventory that no-one wants to buy, but has incurred a
cost 

...and all of that has to be covered by a few hundred dollars profit
margin per unit.

In the good years (late 1990's and even in the dot.com boom time) Compaq
probably increased its PC-oriented staff to handle all this. The problem
is that after 1999 (PC replacements to counter potential Y2K issues) and
2000 (dot com collapse), Compaq continues to employ all these PC people
and incur all these costs.

PC's can make profits (as Dell have shown), but not when you have this
kind of cost burden to support.

Sure Compaq have done a little to reduce inventory and reduce
warehousing costs over the last 12 to 18 months, but it is a drop in the
ocean.

What they need to do is slash the PC-oriented parts of the company by a
big amount (35% or even 50%) and concentrate on the segments that
produce reasonable income ("Enterprise" at about 56%, and Global
services at about 44%).  Reduce the PC warehousing, reduce the
PC-oriented staff (support, sales and admin) and thing will start to
look a whole lot better.

I can understand the reluctance of the Hewlett and Packard families to
continue with the burden of PCs. Sacred cows really have to be
sacrificed !


John McLean


