Compaq's Perkins Says Acquisition Is Misunderstood (Update1)
By Peter J. Brennan

San Francisco, Nov. 9 (Bloomberg) -- Compaq Computer Corp. board member Thomas
Perkins said Hewlett-Packard Co.'s planned purchase of Compaq is opposed by
Hewlett and Packard family members because they don't understand the deal.

``I don't think they know the company or the industry,'' Perkins, founder of
venture-capital firm Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers, said in an interview.
``Neither one has exposure to the inner beauty of Compaq. To reject it before
you know what it's all about -- I don't like it and I was surprised by that.''

Walter Hewlett and David W. Packard, sons of Hewlett-Packard Co.'s co-founders,
this week said they oppose the second-biggest computer maker's planned $20.5
billion purchase of Compaq. Today, Walter Hewlett hired a proxy-solicitation
firm, a sign he will fight the acquisition. Together, the pair along with
siblings and their foundations control about 6.3 percent of the shares.

The David and Lucile Packard Foundation, which controls another 10 percent of
the shares, hasn't decided whether to support the acquisition.
Hewlett-Packard's shares have fallen 18 percent since the acquisition was
announced on Sept. 3. Compaq's have dropped 37 percent.

Hewlett-Packard shares rose 64 cents to $18.99 today. Compaq, the No. 2 maker
of personal computers, fell 26 cents to $7.73.

Hewlett's Response

Perkins said he was about to make presentations to the boards of foundations
linked to the family members to explain the acquisition. He said the pair
should have waited until they received more information.

``I cannot understand why they did it when they did it,'' he said. ``I don't
know why they felt had to do it now.''

Walter Hewlett spokeswoman Joele Frank said Hewlett's foundation has been
advised by outside experts that the acquisition isn't worth it.

``Let's point out that shareholder value declined over $12 billion prior to Mr.
Hewlett's announcement that he was opposed,'' said Frank. ``Given the lack of
stockholder benefits, extensive integration risks are not worth taking.''

Perkins said he was the first general manager of Hewlett- Packard's PC
business, reporting directly to co-founder David Packard. Perkins, whose firm
is one of the best-known venture- capital firms for funding technology
startups, is a co-founder of Compaq and Tandem Computers Inc., which was bought
by Compaq. He said Hewlett-Packard's acquisition of Compaq makes sense.

``The synergies are compelling,'' said Perkins. ``Somewhere between $2 billion
and $3 billion to the bottom line. That's pretty powerful stuff. That's
achieved by a powerful mix of product lines.''

`Sell This Deal'

Perkins praised Hewlett-Packard Chief Executive Officer Carly Fiorina for
having ``a good vision'' and Compaq CEO Michael Capellas for being ``a superb
manager.'' Still, he said the acquisition hasn't been ``well sold'' so far.

``There's an old saying, `When everything else fails, try selling.' We gotta
sell this deal,'' he said. ``We've made a good case to the customers and the
employees. We haven't made a good case to Wall Street although we have made
some progress with analysts.''

Shareholders will eventually approve the acquisition, Perkins predicted.

``I think it will go through,'' he said. ``I don't think it will be easy. It
makes sense. Eventually shareholders and the analysts will wake up to the fact
that the synergies are compelling.

``It would be bad for both companies if it doesn't happen. Walter Hewlett is
voting against his pocketbook.''


