Terry C Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> wrote in message
news:Pine.SGI.4.30.0111102342060.15684-100000@world.std.com...
>
> I just put together a special issue of Shannon Knows Compaq that contains
> three articles on Compaq's IPF Consolidation. Included in the
> free-for-the-downloading PDF are a discussion of why Compaq opted to
> scuttle Alpha, an explanation of why things were done as they were (e.g.
> why publish an Alpha obituary three or four years before the architecture
> shuffles off its mortal coils), and what's behind the current
> unavailability of IPF-based systems.

Despite your introductory statement that "finally details are emerging",
this is largely a re-hash of the bullshit that Compaq (and you) were
spreading so liberally just after June 25th.  Why you expect anyone to find
it any more believable this time around is not clear.

If "Compaq - and Digital before it - invested heavily in a series of
misguided marketing campaigns", they must have been so 'misguided' that the
results appeared only in places like the Australian outback.  Can you point
to any external evidence of such 'heavy' expenditures?  We do know that the
marketing budget for VMS in Y2K was about $13 million (which presumably
includes salaries, though it's not clear who would have merited them), so
the 'heavy' funding sure wasn't spent there.  In the same year Compaq as a
whole spent $385 million on *advertising* (not including things like
salaries and promotions that the term 'marketing budget' encompasses) - and
while Alpha and Tru64 probably received a bit more of that than VMS did
(though I've always suspected that some of the VMS funding, chicken feed
though it was, got siphoned off into more generic Alpha promotion, since you
can buy an awful lot more blinking balls for a few mill than seem to have
gotten handed out) without actual numbers and expense sheets (or copies of
the ads that appeared with indications of where and how often) I'm afraid
I'll just have to file this 'explanation' in the same wastebasket of crap so
many of Compaq's other pronouncements occupy.

Of course, it *is* possible to squander significant amounts of money without
visible effect if you're sufficiently incompetent.  That's one reason why
the magnum opus our group sent to Curly contained specific suggestions in
this area, but we sure as hell know that *those* never got implemented (the
VMS Web pages did improve, but that was  a) already under way and  b) hardly
a significant expenditure).  But Compaq has demonstrated at least some level
of competence in advertising over the years, which makes it a bit difficult
to believe that their Alpha promotional efforts would have been so invisible
had they really been trying.

Not to mention the fact that Alpha remained the corporation's most
profitable franchise.  So if the ad campaigns actually existed and weren't
effective, then, by George, a company interested in profitability would have
tried something else - possibly figuring out that the existing customer base
which was happy to support the products even in the absence of effective
advertising might have a useful suggestion or two.  The explanation offered
to you is pathetic, and your unquestioning acceptance of it equally so.

It is interesting that you say the Alpha 'soul searching' began about two
years ago, since that's about the time of the Pfeiffer/Curly transition.
Which makes one suspect that the decision may have had a hell of a lot more
to do with corporate politics than with anything substantive, but of course
one can't know unless some inside mole chooses to speak.

Then you have the gall to reiterate that "escalating costs and dwindling
performance differentation rendered the Alpha business unsustainable in the
long term".  This is just as much crap as it was when you tried to palm it
off in July:  do you really think people have forgotten why by now, or it is
just the 'big lie' philosophy at work in the hope that the truth has gotten
a bit tired?

Here's a hint:  if costs were indeed escalating, quantify that.  By all
accounts I've heard from the engineers EV8 was on track for both performance
and delivery date, and over the years Alpha development has remained
admirable stable in both progress and costs (save when it's been being
fucked over by upper management) - and has *easily* been justified by the
profits Alpha systems have generated.  I won't repeat my entire July 19th
rebuttal from comp.arch et al. here, but it remains as valid as it did
then - and your contentions remain as vacuous.

And given that Compaq's 'misguided advertising' experience had supposedly
already suggested that it wasn't *just* performance that sold Alphas, that's
a pretty definite indication that some temporary - or even possibly
permanent - erosion in this area would hardly be fatal.  Not that Alpha was
in any danger from IA64 in that area:  POWER4 was and still is the only
credible 64-bit competitor (though Hammer could possibly become one), and
its choice of CMP over SMT could well have left EV8 with advantages in both
chip area and power consumption if not absolute performance.

Moving right along, we get into some really mealy-mouth wording in the EV8
section.  First, you say Compaq concluded that "Alpha would maintain its
performance superiority through the EV7 processor generation", then that the
"EV8 chip was expected to outperform all rivals".  Gee, I guess that means
that Alpha was expected to maintain its performance superiority through EV8,
not just EV7 - i.e., for the foreseeable future.

Then we have "The EV8 program required significant incremental development
resources, and its success was predicated on timely and flawless delivery".
This in the face of an IA64 product that required *humongous* incremental
development resources, was 4 years late (5 years if you don't count Merced
because it's a joke that no one is using commercially), and nothing like the
processing powerhouse it was promoted as:  if Intel can justify continued
IA64 development, then there's no way in hell that Compaq can't justify
developing as good a product as Alpha.

I really hope that one of the former Alpha engineers will address your
'anecdotal evidence' that SMT would have cost a full GHz:  I certainly never
heard mention of any problem of that sort and I certainly wouldn't trust
your Compaq sources to know, let alone be truthful, about it.

And your artful juxtaposition in the phrase "EV8 was projected to have a
marginal advantage over then-current IBM and Intel processors" conveniently
obscures the fact that the *only* Intel processors able to make Alpha break
a sweat are, and would have continued to be, 32-bit processors, not Itanic:
EV6 makes Merced a joke (have you forgotten the 'smoking brick of death'?),
EV7 will make McKinley a joke (any lessening of the difference in SPECint
numbers will be more than erased in real-world commercial performance by
EV7's on-chip memory and MP support), and Intel hasn't even unveiled *plans*
for a processor beyond the McKinley/Madison/Deerfield core (so we can't
specify what EV8 would have made a joke of, though the Alpha team transplant
eventually may help at least reduce the volume of the laughter).

By contrast, the 'Chips are Down' section actually raises some valid points,
but then proceeds to completely misinterpret their significance.  It is
indeed the case that "modern servers are increasingly differentiated by
metrics such as latency, bandwidth, RAS features, NUMA implementation, and
system balance" - but EV7, due to its on-chip glue, has *vast* superiority
in latency, bandwidth, NUMA performance, and system balance over any Itanic
yet talked about by Intel, which translates to at least a 3-year head-start
in those areas over any IA64-based server.  And had you included 'power
consumption' in the list as you should have, Alpha's continuing advantage is
even greater.  But now, Compaq must compete either with a dead-ended Alpha
platform or a laughable Itanic platform against POWER4 on the high end and
Hammer on the low end, both of which have on-chip glue similar to EV7's and
reasonable power consumption.

But it's back to weasel-wording in the next section, where you have the gall
to suggest that Compaq evaluated Itanic and POWER4 in anything resembling an
even-handed manner and found POWER4 wanting because, even though Itanic is a
complete slug by virtue of massively-flawed design assumptions that will
weigh it down forever in commercial (vs. floating-point-style) applications,
"Intel is an undisputed leader in process technology".  The use of the word
'an' keeps the rationale merely completely misleading rather than an
outright lie - since of course IBM is also 'an undisputed leader in process
technology', so when comparing Itanic to POWER4 that's a wash rather than
any telling Intel advantage.

And finally we have 'the essence of Alpha'.  If Intel will indeed "leverage
Compaq's expertise in glueless multiprocessing, high bandwidth and low
latency CPU designs, symmetric multithreading, and chip-based
multiprocessing", then all the more reason to believe that *Compaq* could
have leveraged them in Alpha effectively as well - with at least a 3-year
head-start over their potential appearance on the decks of the Itanic.  And
since POWER4 and Hammer appear to have about the same lead in several of
these areas, Compaq is now left without *any* competitive platform to field
(unless they can convince people to commit to a declared-dying
architecture).

Saying that "the game plan appears valid and defensible" sounds a lot like a
3-year-old pronouncing the likely outcome of the next Presidential election
based on his parents' political leanings:  he may know the names of the
candidates, but he has no clue about the mechanisms involved.  So while
you've done a decent job of taking Compaq's June rationalizations and
dressing them up in soothing and superficially-convincing verbiage, it's
still utter crap.  If you believe it, you're a fool; if not, you're a pimp.

I won't go into detailed deconstruction of your "Hasta la vista" article,
since it's based on the flawed premise that the migration off Alpha makes
some kind of sense and just goes down-hill from there.  But I will ask you
to identify any Intel commitment to include *any* enhancements to Itanic
(which you seem to think are important upsides of the deal for Compaq -
though again this assumes that having VMS and Tru64 run on Itanic is
worthwhile in the first place) that will make life easier for Compaq's OSs:
I've seen absolutely no indication that Intel wants anything but to apply
the expertise of the Alpha team to keeping Itanic afloat, nor do I know of
any specific tweaks that would be much help to VMS or Tru64 anyway (and
Itanic already incorporates the lock-step facilities that NSK needs).  And I
will take exception to your characterization of EV8 as "a classic example of
trouble waiting to happen":  that sounds a lot more like a classic example
of an infomercial masquerading as journalism.

- bill





