Jimbond <jimbond@earthlink.net> writes:
> I'm replacing the CDrom on a VS3100 (RD40 ??) with a Pannasonic CR-503-B
> model, But I havent been able to find a listing of what the jumpers should
> be set to
> for this VAX. I know the block size must be set and with only two positions
> I got a
> 50/50 chance of getting it right, but there are jumpers for other things as
> well.
> If anyone has put one of these CDroms in a VaxStation could you let me know
> were you found the settings or send me a cheat sheet ??  TIA

  You probably mean an RRD40. That's a weird drive with a slide-in caddy
with "mandible-like" CD retention, unlike the more common Sony/NEC flip
caddys that were popular.

  However, the basic RRD40 used a LMSI serial interface, not SCSI. There
were some SCSI ones, but I've only seen them in Infoservers with outboard
bridge boards. Make sure you have a SCSI one before proceeding.

  The general jumpers you'll find on older SCSI devices are:

  1) SCSI ID (normally 3 jumpers)
  2) Parity enable/disable (1 jumper)
  3) TERMPWR (termination power) enable/disable (1 jumper)

  There will also likely be one, two, or 3 removable termination resistor
packs.

  Disk drives may have a motor start jumper, and CD-ROMs might have a sec-
tor size jumper (but not always).

  You can set any ID that isn't already used (don't forget the controller
takes an ID as well). If you know your old drive's ID, use that if you are
replacing it.

  For the VS3100, enable parity and remove terminators.

  Set sector size 512 if you can. If you can't, and the drive accepts this
in a mode page *and* you don't plan on booting from it *and* you have a
recent version of VMS, VMS will do the mode set when it accesses the drive.

  As usual, both the Compaq folks and I will tell you that random SCSI wid-
gets may or may not work. Plextor makes the PX-40TSi which is a 40x drive
with specific support for VMS. Street price a little over $100 new.

