0f8a0  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 ................
........ trailing zeros....

Apparently, first two or four bytes are a checksum, because changing a
single byte does not work. Once the system is rebooted, the isacfg stuff
sees that its table is corrupt and rewrites it. Then, apparently the
checksum is applied again, trashing the ISA table. Consequently, isacfg
ALWAYS complains about a corrupt table. Sigh.
Downgrading to SRM 4.9 and then doing a isacfg -init seems to write the
ISA table at a different address or in a different style, which is taken
into account by the newer SRMs.

Could perhaps some dear soul with a PC164 dump its eerom on FAT
formatted floppy in SRM like this:
>>>cat eerom > fat:eerom.dmp/dva0

and send me the 64KB EEROM.DMP file? (It probably just uses the the
upper 2KB, so 
>>>cat eerom:f800 > fat:eerom.dmp/dva0
seems to be enough, but who knows.)

It seems that doing the reverse will overwrite and flash the ROM in the
proper way. Perhaps that will help, as I'm still stuck because neither
fwupdate.exe nor going to pc164nt.rom will work with this particular
board.

