Write-through writes data immediately to disk.  Write-back is more aggressive.
It holds data in the controller memory it needs the memory to hold some newer
data.

If the cache is bad, as you suspect, the 72-pin SIMMs used in the Mylex 960
series are fast page mode with parity.  I've seen controllers with 4MB, 16MB,
and 32MB SIMMs.  70 ns. or faster, tin-plated, I think.

Is the controller EISA or PCI?  Which model is it?  A replacement controller is
not out of the question, either... Ben Myers 

