The RQDX3 doesn't modify the command reference on the way out.  It
actually creates the end packet in the same physical buffer as the
original saved SetContChar packet.  (The RQDX3 has a T11 running a
C/assembler PDP11 program as microcode.)  After pulling out the host
timeout value and the error log flags (the only settable values), it
sets up these following fields:

status = success
controller flags = new controller flags
timeout = 120
software and hardware versions
max byte count = 0,0
controller options = 0,0,0,type
opcode = scc end
type = sequential
size = #bytes in packet

and fires the result back.

This is very typical of the RQDX3.  It maintains an internal queue of
packets and uses them to store commands from the host.  For immediate
commands, it constructs the end response in the command packet,
thereby guaranteeing the availability of a response packet in its
internal queue.  Obviously, data transfer packets, and other queued
oeprations, go through a more complicated sequence.

/Bob

