Hey Folks and Bob:

Well, thank you for information, Bob Sunpik...  I know that RQDX3 disk
controller has T11 processor.  However, I still have some RQDX3 emulation
problems.  After received the end message from RQDX3, OpenVMS did nothing
afterward until timeout alarm set off and restarted entire initialization
again and again...

In "Set Controller Characteristics End Message", I carefully studied
traced executions but they looks like fine.  I found nothing is
wrong with my RQDX3 emulator.  OpenVMS still am waiting for unit
status information like DUA0:, etc.

About the last fail message, I do not know about message header information.
OpenVMS did requested the last fail message per LF bit set in step #4
initialization.  I assumed that last fail message's header information:

   Virtual circuit ID  = 0
   Message Type        = 1 (Error Datagram)
   Credits             = 0

OpenVMS received the last fail message but did nothing with it but
just labeled my RQDX3 emulator as "SABKUP$PUA:".  By using VC ID#,
OpenVMS jumped into one of functions.  It ended up the "null" function at
est. location 80C71111 before it labeled "PUA:".  Yes, I found SCSNODE
in SYSBOOT> prompt.  That's why It said that its SCSNODE variable is
"SABKUP".  I assume that last fail message is suspect that cause
possible 'derailment' of RQDX3 initialization.

Also I noticed that unknown bit in step #4 of initialization.  OpenVMS
sent '002003' to SA register.   Does anyone know about that bit (2)
in '002003'?  My MQDX documentation does not have that information.
Yes, MQDX has complete information about entire sequence initialization.

Then OpenVMS took a nap until timer alarm set off for next initialization.
It finally send the first MSCP command to my RQDX3 emulator.  It
processed and sent it back to OpenVMS to continue.  OpenVMS successfully,
normally processed the end message.  Then OpenVMS took another nap
until the timeout alarm set off.  Then OpenVMS restarted the
entire initialization again and again!

I learned that OpenVMS is senstive to message header like virtual circuit
ID, message type, and credits.  Specific settings are required to process
properly.  Also, I discovered that OpenVMS is complete interrupt-driven
operating system with multi-tier IPL levels.  OpenVMS uses IPL 8 to process
device interrupt services from multi-level Q22 interrupts (200 to 3F4).
According to my book, I learned that there are groups of vectors can be
specifically to IPL interrupt.  For example, 3C0 to 3F4 is IPL BR7 (17), etc..  Does anyone have any suggestions?

Any solutions about that problems??  It happened nothing after
first MSCP command. :-(

Thank you!

-- Tim Stark

