In alt.sys.pdp10 Kevin Handy <kth@srv.net> wrote:
>> About the last fail message, I do not know about message header information.
>> OpenVMS did requested the last fail message per LF bit set in step #4
>> initialization.  I assumed that last fail message's header information:

> Isn't the last-fail packet only send when an error occurs? If so, then
> that might be what is triggering it to restart.  It might be trying
> several times to see if it stabilizes.

Hmmm. When I suspended the last-fail packet from being sent during step 
4, it resulted the same.  I will remove it from step 4 initialization
until it is sent if serious error occurs....

>> Then OpenVMS took a nap until timer alarm set off for next initialization.
>> It finally send the first MSCP command to my RQDX3 emulator.  It
>> processed and sent it back to OpenVMS to continue.  OpenVMS successfully,
>> normally processed the end message.  Then OpenVMS took another nap
>> until the timeout alarm set off.  Then OpenVMS restarted the
>> entire initialization again and again!

> It sounds like the VAX is not seeing any interrupts generated by the
> RQDX3.  In step 1, if the IE bit is set (bit 7) then it expects that
> the RQDX will send an interrupt after it sets up for each step.

> Or is it sleeping after it gets the interrupt and before going on
> to the next step? I don't know why it would do that, since all the
> example code (PDP11 based) that I've seen loops through it without
> any pause.

No, each step initialization, VAX did received an interrupt after MTPR
instruction changed IPL to 8.  All steps for initialization were
completed successfully without any pause.  Then it went to sleep before 
it started to send STCON command to RQDX3.

During step 4,  OpenVMS received 040463 (Model 19, Revision 3) and
processed it by labeling it as PUA: device.  When it was done, it exited 
through REI and SIRR 8 was interrupted immediately after REI was 
executed.  It checked for any incoming packet but found nothing.  Then it 
went sleep for some time.  When it woke up, it sent STCON command message 
to RQDX3.  Then RQDX3 set up controller flags and sent results back to 
VAX and issued an interrupt to wake up.

When VAX received an interript, OpenVMS woke up and checked for any new
packets.  It found the results from RQDX3 to process.  It did processed
the end STCON message packet.  Then it went back to sleep forever until
timeout alarm set off.  It did not send any ONLINE commands.

Today, I tried Available Attention Message but it did not work.  After
STCON command was processed, RQDX3 tried to send an attention packet
to VAX.  VAX received an interrupt and processed it.  It recongized the
Available Message packet and checked some settings in somewhere.  It
found out and tossed it away.  It went back to sleep without send
ONLINE commands. :-(

-- Tim Stark

